Chapter 1
Introduction
The objective of this thesis is to present novel methodologies to deal with epistemic uncertainty in engineering design activities of emerging technologies, such as nuclear fusion
power plants. These activities often involve the following questions (not necessarily in this
particular order):
1. What design variables are the most important?
2. What is the best design?
3. Is the design safe?
The first question is framed in the context of uncertainty quantification and sensitivity analysis.
Finding answers to this question helps scientists and engineers to identify which design
variables require further investigation, or to find in which design areas to put a particular
focus. The second question, as the adjective best suggests, is clearly related to design
optimisation activities. Optimisation is about finding the design that maximises certain figure
of merit, such as performance, while satisfying constraints (e.g. costs, resources, physics,
time, etc.). Lastly, risk analyses are in charge of answering the question of safety. The
system should be reliable enough to operate as intended, should not pose a risk to people,
and comply with the required regulation.
The methods employed to answer these questions are often based on mathematical models
and computational simulations [1]. At present, engineers make use of the physical theories,
empirical data, and expert knowledge to build models and perform simulations that can
produce precise predictions of the system response. However, these models are abstractions
of the reality, and they are affected by uncertainties of different nature. These uncertainties
can be classified, in general, into two main categories: aleatory and epistemic. The former is
related to the inherent randomness of phenomena, and is irreducible. The latter, of which

2 Introduction
this thesis is concerned with, is related to the lack of knowledge about the system, and can be
reduced with more information.
Mature engineering systems (such as aircraft, automobiles, bridges, and similar well
established systems), as opposed to emerging technologies, have access to an extensive
amount of knowledge due to the experience that has been gathered after many years of
research and production. It can be argued that epistemic uncertainty has little impact on
these engineering systems and design activities do not necessarily require accounting for
it. However, that is not the case for the technologies involved in a nuclear fusion power
plant. Designing and building a fusion power plant is a completely new endeavour, and
there are many epistemic uncertainties regarding its design variables, the design itself,
and the consequences of a failure. It is true that experimental fusion reactors have been
built before, but a fusion power plant is a remarkably different engineering feat since the
operating conditions will be significantly harsher than in current reactors. For example, little
is known about building and operating breeder blankets, which are the components that
will breed tritium to fuel the power plant. As of today, no breeder blanket has been tested
and integrated in a fusion reactor, and their design and performance are subject to severe
epistemic uncertainty.
Aleatory and epistemic uncertainties are often treated in the same way, and the same
mathematical tools are used to quantify them. However, as will be discussed, each are
fundamentally different in nature and should be treated differently. Not doing so can lead
to misleading interpretations of the results, and to wrong decisions [2]. By distinguishing
between aleatory and epistemic uncertainty, analysts and decision makers can improve the
interpretation of modelling and simulation results and develop strategies to reduce system
response uncertainty when both types are present. In recent years, there has been a growing
interest in the appropriate treatment of epistemic uncertainty [3]. This interest has also
reached the nuclear fusion community, which now also acknowledges its importance in the
context of fusion power plant design [4]. These are the reasons for the relevance of this
thesis; firstly, in its broad scope of dealing with epistemic uncertainty in engineering design
activities, and secondly, in its particular application to nuclear fusion.
This thesis is organised into six chapters. The first two chapters will serve as a theoretical
background underlying the contribution of this work. Chapter 1 is a general introduction of
the problems that this thesis is attempting to tackle. A brief introduction to nuclear fusion
is provided, followed by the progress in fusion research made to date. Then, it is discussed
how probability theory was introduced in engineering design activities, and how it is used
today in some engineering problems with the aid of computational simulations. Lastly,
a description of the differences between aleatory and epistemic uncertainties is provided,

1.1 Nuclear fusion 3
explaining why they must be treated differently. Chapter 2 explains the fundamentals of
magnetic confinement fusion, the European DEMOstration fusion power plant [5], and the
specific challenges this thesis focuses on. Then, the following three chapters will present the
main contributions of this thesis:
• Chapter 3 presents a methodology to perform uncertainty quantification and sensitivity
analysis with imprecise probabilities, and shows an application to nuclear fusion power
plants.
• Chapter 4 shows that nuclear data uncertainties have an impact in breeder blanket
design, and introduces a method to deal with those uncertainties in design optimisation
activities.
• Chapter 5 introduces a method to employ imprecise probabilities in the context of risk
analysis, with an application to a pressure tank system.
Lastly, Chapter 6 discusses the conclusions of this thesis and the future work that could
be done.
1.1 Nuclear fusion
Nuclear fusion is the process in which lighter atomic nuclei fuse to form heavier, more stable
nuclei. The stability of nuclei is related to their binding energy, which is the energy that
holds the nucleus constituents together or, in other words, the energy required to separate
them. Figure 1.1 shows the average binding energy per nucleon in MeV as a function of
the mass number. The average binding energy per nucleon shows a peak in the region near
nickel; meaning that either the fission of heavier nuclei or the fusion of lighter nuclei will
produce nuclei that are more tightly bound.
When fusion reactions occur, the products will have smaller masses than the sum of the
reactant’s masses, but higher binding energy. This mass deficit, also called mass defect,
results in the release of energy. A common fusion reaction is 2H-3H fusion, or deuteriumtritium (D-T) fusion
2H+3H → 4He (3.5MeV) +n(14.1MeV) .
The products of this reaction are an alpha particle and a neutron, together holding 17.6 MeV
of kinetic energy as a consequence of the mass defect in the reaction. Notably, the neutron
carries four-fifths of this energy, while the alpha particle carries the remaining one-fifth.

