In the case of the D-T fusion reaction, the interaction radius is r ≈ 3.24×10−15m, which
yields a Coulomb barrier of 443 keV or 5.14×109 K.1
Fortunately, for a fusion reaction to occur colliding energies do not have to be exactly the
required to overcome the Coulomb’s barrier for two reasons: quantum tunnelling and the
particles in the Maxwellian tail. In fact, there is a fusion reaction probability distribution as a
function of the colliding energies of the nuclei. These functions are called cross-sections and
are expressed in barns (10−24 cm2). Figure 1.2 shows the fusion reaction cross sections for
different nuclides. From the figure, it is possible to infer that D-T fusion has an advantage
over the other fusion reactions: it has the highest probability of occurring whilst requiring the
least energy, peaking at around 70 keV. This is why D-T fusion is one of the most promising
reactions for producing energy in a reactor.2
In 1920, Arthur Eddington hypothesised that the energy source which powers the stars
was produced by the fusion of small nuclei, and that the same process could be controlled for
power generation [8]:
If, indeed, the sub-atomic energy in the stars is being freely used to maintain
their great furnaces, it seems to bring a little nearer to fulfilment our dream of
controlling this latent power for the well-being of the human race—or for its
suicide.
Almost 20 years later, in 1939, Hans Bethe solved the puzzle of the stellar nucleosynthesis
with the clues that Eddington provided [9].3 The first demonstration of a fusion reaction in
a laboratory was achieved in 1934 by Mark Oliphant, Paul Harteck and Ernest Rutherford,
when they used an accelerator to fire deuterium at different elements. For them, “the most
interesting and important reaction was that of deuterium on deuterium itself” [10]. They were
surprised to find that when bombarding deuterium on deuterium “an enormous effect was
1This would be the temperature required to provide 444 keV as an average thermal energy for each particle.
To put this number in perspective, the temperature at the core of the Sun is about 15×106 K.
2Other reactions, such as 3He-3He or p-11B fusion, may have different advantages over D-T fusion despite
being more challenging to achieve.
3The work of Atkinson and Houtermans on the calculations of the rate of nuclear fusion in stars in 1929,
and the mathematical foundations of quantum tunnelling introduced by George Gamow in 1928, alongside the
explanation of the proton-proton chain reaction by Gamow and von Weizsäcker in 1938 were also essential for
Bethe.

6 Introduction
Fig. 1.2 Cross section of different fusion reactions. Adapted from [6].

1.1 Nuclear fusion 7
produced”. They assumed that what they measured was an effect due to radiation passing
through the counting chamber, but in fact what they observed was a very large emission of
protons and neutrons. They just witnessed deuterium-deuterium fusion, being
2H+2H → 3H(1.01MeV) +1H(3.02MeV)
2H+2H → 3He (0.82MeV) +n(2.45MeV),
where tritium and 3He were also discovered.
A boom in nuclear fusion research happened in the 1950s; not because of the detonation
of Ivy Mike, the first test of a thermonuclear device [11], but because most of the modern
fusion reactor concepts and designs were envisaged then. The tokamak in 1950 by Andrei
Sakharov and Igor Tamm,4the stellarator by Lyman Spitzer in 1951, and different kinds of
pinch devices such as ZETA and Scylla, which dominated the experimental scene. In fact,
Scylla I achieved the first controlled fusion reaction in a laboratory [12]. The experimental
advancements in fusion research remained secret due to the Cold War until the United
Nations Conference in Geneva, in 1958, where the large variety of developments from several
laboratories were brought together [13].
The fabrication of fusion reactors proliferated with new configurations and variants until
progress stalled in the mid-1960s. The way forward was shown by the Soviet tokamak
programme led by Lev Artsimovich during the late 1960s; the magnetic configuration of the
tokamak precluded the worst instabilities present in pinch devices, allowing a better confinement of the reactions. A major breakthrough was presented during the Third International
Conference on Plasma Physics and Controlled Nuclear Fusion Research in 1968, where
Artsimovich’s team showed groundbreaking results in their T3 tokamak, which claimed
it achieved electron temperatures up to 1 keV (11.6×106 K) during more than 10 ms [14].
These results were received with scepticism in the West since previous records were of the
order of 100 eV and 1 ms (i.e. ten times lower), and five scientists from the United Kingdom
Atomic Energy Authority (UKAEA) were invited to travel to the Kurchatov Institute to
replicate the experiments. The results were indeed correct [15], and gave green light to the
development of tokamak reactors.
Since the successful results of the T3 tokamak, many improvements and discoveries
have been made. The Princeton Large Torus (PLT), operated between 1975 and 1986, set
several records and helped to demonstrate that large scale reactors had better performances
overall [16]. The high-confinement mode, which is an operating mode that allows better
performances and longer pulse lengths, was discovered in ASDEX in 1982 [17]. The START
4They were initially motivated by a letter by Oleg Lavrentiev.

8 Introduction
reactor demonstrated higher pressures in tighter designs in 1991 [18]. Tore Supra, in 1996,
achieved a pulse length of two minutes [19] (recall that, 30 years before, achieving pulses
of 10 milliseconds was difficult to believe). In 1983 the construction of the Joint European
Torus (JET) was completed, a tokamak that later in 1997 would set a record by producing
22 MJ of thermal energy [20]. This achievement remained unsurpassed until 2023, when JET
once again broke its own record by producing 69 MJ of heat [21], as depicted in Figure 1.3.
Fig. 1.3 Records in fusion pulses at JET. ©EUROfusion / United Kingdom Atomic Energy
Authority.
To give a glimpse of how the performance of fusion reactors has improved since the T3
tokamak, Figure 1.4 shows the improvement in the fusion triple product until 1998. This
triple product, which is an extension of the Lawson criterion [22], is an important figure of
merit employed to measure reactor performance, as it is the product of the plasma density n,
temperature T, and energy confinement time τE. The triple product will be later developed in
the Chapter 2 of this thesis, as it is a crucial criterion in fusion power plant design.
To date, a few tokamaks are still under operation, each with their own characteristics,
helping to shed light on the challenges of harnessing fusion power as a practical energy
source. Yet, there are some fundamental questions in plasma physics and fusion engineering
that remain unanswered, hindering the progress towards a fusion power plant. ITER, which
sits at the top right of Figure 1.4, is currently being built in southern France and will help
to answer some of those fundamental questions [24]. ITER is designed to approach the

1.1 Nuclear fusion 9
Fig. 1.4 Evolution of the fusion triple product in different tokamaks over the years. The line
shows a polynomial fit excluding ITER. Adapted from [6] with data from [23].

10 Introduction
conditions necessary for a fusion power plant, significantly surpassing the capabilities of any
existing machine, although it will not fully achieve power plant conditions.
Building and operating fusion reactors are costly and time consuming enterprises; it would
be prohibitively expensive to build and test every single fusion power plant that is proposed
and analysed. Nevertheless, digital models can help to mitigate this problem. With the
recent advances in computer-aided modelling and in silico experimentation, it is possible to
build digital models to study and assess the performance of hypothetical fusion power plants
before turning them into a reality. In fact, computational simulation is one of the essential
activities in current fusion research. Data gathered from experimental reactors are combined
with predictive theories in computational models to help to understand the underlying
physics and to design future reactors. These are the objectives of the European Integrated
Tokamak Modelling Task Force (EFDA ITM-TF) [25, 26], which made available “a set
of validated simulation tools, accessible and useful for ITER prediction and interpretation
activity, as well as for the conceptual design of the future reactors”. Achieving trustworthy
predictive capability requires computational models that have shown to be valid. However,
validating fusion models involves complexities beyond the usual challenges: nonlinearities,
multiple scales, different reactor geometries and characteristics, and interest to extrapolate to
unexplored regimes [27, 28].
1.2 Modelling and simulation in fusion engineering design
Computers are essential tools in modern science and engineering, as in silico experimentation
with computational models enables the reproduction of experimental results from theories and
provides insights about processes and phenomena that may be experimentally inaccessible.
In engineering design, computational models are used in different ways to evaluate the
robustness, performance, and safety of systems. One possibility is to employ these models to
supplement the results of physical tests and guide test-based engineering. An example of this
application is the use of computational models to predict the performance of a new aircraft
design, and then use this information to guide the design of the physical prototype [29].
Computational models can also be used to assess the robustness, performance, and safety of
systems when these cannot be tested in fully representative environments and scenarios [1].
Some applications of this kind are found in the safety analyses of nuclear power reactors and
nuclear waste disposal facilities. The former possibility is often found in mature engineering
systems, where the computational models are considered to be sufficiently accurate to be used
to make actionable predictions and physical prototyping is affordable. The latter, however,
is often found in technologies with little or no empirical evidence where experimentation

1.2 Modelling and simulation in fusion engineering design 11
is not possible, such as fusion power plants. Nevertheless, fusion power plant modelling
and simulation is extremely challenging due to the complexity of the processes involved, the
computational cost of the simulations, and the lack of empirical evidence at reactor relevant
conditions.
Computational models are essentially composed of mathematical functions, usually
expressed in terms of differential or integral equations that represent the behaviour of a
system. As mentioned before, these models will be an abstraction of reality, and therefore,
they will not be able to capture all the details of the system for different reasons. One
possibility is that the system is too complex to be modelled in its entirety, and therefore, some
simplifications are required. For example, the Liouville’s equation describes the evolution of
a particle distribution function of N interacting particles
fN = fN(q1, p1,...,qN, pN,t) , (1.5)
where qi and pi are the position and momentum of the i-th particle, respectively, and t is time.
The Liouville’s equation is governed by the following dimensionality equation,
