where m is the mass of the particles and Fiis the force acting on the i-th particle. This equation
is a function of 6N +1 variables (3N Cartesian coordinates, 3N momentum components, and
time), and therefore, it is impractical to solve it for a large number of particles.5In such
cases, where the system is too complex to be simulated, it is necessary to reduce the model by
making assumptions. This problem is exacerbated when the system is composed of several
sub-systems, each one representing a different physical phenomenon at different scales.
This multi-physics, multi-scale modelling is present in fusion power plant design, where
phenomena are acting at different spatial (10−6 −102m) and temporal scales (10−10 −104s),
and a great amount of computation is required to perform simulation [30, 31].
Ideally, a model should be built from first principles; meaning that it has been derived
from physical laws due to a fundamental understanding of the underlying phenomena.
For example, the Navier-Stokes equations can be derived from the conservation of mass,
momentum, energy, and the assumption of a Newtonian fluid. However, sometimes there is a
lack of understanding of the theory, and the models are built using regression methods relying
solely on empirical evidence. This situation is found in some aspects of fusion power plant
5The density in a tokamak plasma is in the order of 1020 particles per cubic metre. ITER it is designed for a
plasma volume of 840m3.

12 Introduction
design, as in the L-H transition from low confinement mode (L-mode) to high confinement
mode (H-mode). The L-H transition is characterised by a sudden increase in the energy
confinement time, and therefore, it is of great interest for designing better-performing reactors.
However, the underlying physics of this transition is not well understood, and therefore, the
models used to predict the L-H transition are based on empirical data [32]. Another example
is the prediction of the energy confinement time6. Heat and particle transport in tokamak
reactors are governed by complex processes that are not yet fully understood, and energy
confinement time is highly dependent on them. Since it is not yet possible to provide a first
principles derivation of the dependence of the energy confinement time on the reactor and
plasma parameters, regression methods are employed on databases of experimental results to
obtain empirical formulas [33].
Another difficulty in tokamak modelling arises as a consequence of conditions and
regimes in current experiments which are notably different from those of future reactors,
and extrapolation of current machine data to reactors is required. For example, despite
D-T fusion being the more promising reaction for electricity generation, most experiments
conducted in tokamaks have been performed without D-T plasmas. The reasons are that
D-T plasmas produce highly energetic neutrons that would damage and activate the reactor
components, complicating the reactor upgrade, maintenance, and the experimentation and
decommissioning activities. Furthermore, tritium is a valuable, radioactive isotope that
requires special licensing and handling, and therefore, it is not ideal for experimenting with.
Instead, many experiments have been carried out with deuterium plasmas due to the similarity
in mass to D-T plasmas. However, there are significant differences between deuterium
plasmas and D-T plasmas, since the latter will produce fusion-born alpha particles that will
drastically change the plasma dynamics. These dynamics are not yet fully understood either;
hence, the extrapolation from deuterium plasmas to D-T plasmas remains a challenge [34, 35].
The modelling and simulation activities for fusion power plant design pose an unparalleled
challenge, as these require reducing highly complex models with interacting sub-systems
governed by different physics at different scales, where first principles are not always
available and need extrapolation due to the lack of data.
1.3 Uncertainty in engineering design
In modern engineering design, the measured properties of materials and components are
employed alongside appropriate predictive theories to create systems that meet specific
6Energy confinement time is one of the key figures of merit of magnetic confinement fusion, and measures
the rate at which the plasma loses energy to its environment.

1.3 Uncertainty in engineering design 13
requirements: accurate measured properties are combined with theory to produce precise
predictions. For example, a structure could be designed to provide a desired factor of safety
relative to a specified maximum load SS/SL, where SL is the load and SS the structural strength.
Statistics and probability were incorporated in engineering design as it was recognised that
neither the load nor the structural strength could be determined precisely [36].
A computational model is a representation of a system or a process in the form of mathematical functions f : X → Y, where the input is a vector of parameters x = (x1, x2,..., xn)
and the output is a vector of responses y = (y1, y2,..., ym). The previous illustration with
perfectly known loads and structural strength is an example of a deterministic model. In that
case, it would be possible to say whether the structure will ever fail for a given load or not
(e.g. if SS > SL the structure will never fail).7 The case where the loads and the structural
strength are assumed to be uncertain parameters is an example of a stochastic model. In
that situation, it is not possible to say whether the structure will ever fail for a given load
or not, but it is possible to calculate the probability of failure. These cases are depicted in
Figure 1.5, where the deterministic model is represented by single points and the stochastic
model by probability functions. In the deterministic case, since SS > SL it could be said that
the structure will never fail due to this load and the analysis would stop there; on the other
hand, in the stochastic case there is a probability of failure. This probability can be calculated
as
Pfailure =
