1.3 Uncertainty in engineering design 15
Uncertainty quantification and sensitivity analysis are perhaps the fundamental activities
when dealing with computational models and uncertainty. The objective of uncertainty
quantification is to measure the impact of the different sources of uncertainty on the output
of a model [39]. On the other hand, sensitivity analysis investigates the relationships
between the inputs and outputs [40]. These activities are crucial for reporting the uncertainty
in the model predictions and essential for its trustworthiness. Uncertainty quantification
and sensitivity analysis often are performed together with the following purposes: model
verification and validation, model calibration, model reduction, and decision-making. Model
verification and validation are the processes of determining whether the model is correctly
implemented from the concept (i.e. it does what is planned to do), and whether the model
is an accurate representation of the real system, respectively [1, 41]. In model calibration,
the parameters are adjusted to improve the agreement between the model predictions and
the experimental data [42]. Model reduction has the objective of simplifying the model to
reduce its computational cost [43]. Practical problems from engineering can easily involve
models with an unmanageable number of input parameters (as in Liouville’s equation), and
identifying the most important parameters can help to find a simpler model that is easier to
solve. Finally, knowing the input parameters with the highest impact on the model response
can help to make research and design decisions.
Another application of probabilistic methods in engineering is in design optimisation
under uncertainty [44]. Optimisation is an essential activity in engineering design, as it is
employed to find the design that maximises or minimises a given performance measure subject
to constraints. When the performance measure and the constraints are deterministic functions
of the design variables, the problem is called deterministic optimisation, and uncertainty
plays no role. In this case, performance measure and constraints would be constant for
a given design. However, it is commonly accepted that uncertainty is inevitable at every
stage of the life cycle of an engineering system, and designs found through deterministic
methods tend to be very risky or over-conservative. Stochastic optimisation, on the other
hand, takes uncertainty into account, and the performance measure and/or constraints are
stochastic functions of the design variables. Figure 1.6 depicts the difference between
deterministic and stochastic optimisation. In this example, when uncertainty is considered in
the design found with deterministic methods, chances of not complying with the constraints
are about 0.75. Instead, when stochastic optimisation is employed, the probability of failure
is negligible. The solution obtained through stochastic optimisation is more reliable than its
deterministic counterpart because, despite variations due to uncertainties, it still complies
with the constraints.

16 Introduction
Fig. 1.6 Design trade-off in reliability-based design optimisation. About 75% of the designs
around fail to satisfy the constraints. Adapted from [? ].
There are diverse approaches for stochastic optimisation, including expectation minimisation, minimisation of variance, and minimisation of maximum costs [44]. A type of stochastic
optimisation is reliability-based optimisation, which allows solving optimisation problems
while modelling directly the effects of uncertainty [45]. However, this method can be computationally demanding since it involves calculating expected values and probabilities inside
the optimisation algorithm. Therefore, techniques for solving reliability-based optimisation
problems require the application of approximate methods [46], advanced simulation [47], or
metamodelling techniques [48].
Lastly, probabilistic techniques also appear in the risk assessments of engineering systems.
While the concept of risk can be controversial with its meaning varying across disciplines,
in its quantitative sense risk is commonly defined as "the statistical expected value of an
unwanted event that may or may not occur” [49]. Thus, calculating risks involves identifying
potential hazards and measuring their probabilities and consequences [50]. However, the
appropriate method for characterising, representing, propagating, and interpreting these
probabilities and consequences continue to be a subject of debate [51]. In the nuclear power
industry, probabilistic risk assessment is an essential activity for designing and operating
of nuclear power plants [52, 53]. An example of probabilistic risk assessment is fault tree
analysis, developed by Bell Laboratories and used in the production of the systems controlling
the launch of Minuteman missiles [54]. In fault tree analysis, the system is represented as
a tree, where the top event is the system failure, and the bottom events are the basic events
that can cause the system failure. Boolean operators, such as the AND and OR gates, are
employed to connect the basic events and calculate the probability of failure of the system.

1.3 Uncertainty in engineering design 17
Figure 1.7 shows an example of a simple fault tree with A, B, and C as basic events and
T as the top event. Knowing the failure rate of A, B, and C, it is possible to calculate the
probability of failure of T.
OR
B AND T
A
C
Fig. 1.7 Simple fault tree with A, B, and C being the basic events and T being the top event.
The basic mathematical operations associated with the AND and OR gates are the product
and the sum, respectively. The AND gate represents the combination of independent events
P(A and B) = P(A)·P(B) , (1.9)
while the OR gate is the union set of the events8
P(A or B) = P(A) +P(B)−P(A)·P(B) . (1.10)
The probability of failure of the top event in Figure 1.7 is then
P(T) = P(P(C) and P(A or B)) = P(C)·P(A or B) = P(C)·(P(A) +P(B)) . (1.11)
In engineering systems, it is usual that the probability of failure of a component is derived
from its frequency of failure obtained from operational performance data. The frequency of
failure of a component is the number of times that the component fails in a given period of
time. However, it is also usual to assume certain distributions for the frequency of failure
of a component, such as the exponential distribution, which requires the failure rate to be
constant with time [55]. Activities such as fault tree analysis motivate a systematic approach
towards designing complex systems and offer crucial technical information by highlighting
design flaws and sensitivities [56].
8The product term in the OR gate is often ignored when the probabilities of individual events are small. This
practice is grounded in the assumption of statistical independence and the mathematical properties of small
probabilities.

18 Introduction
1.4 Epistemic uncertainty
Uncertainty is typically categorised into two kinds: aleatory and epistemic. Aleatory uncertainty, also referred to as variability or stochasticity, stems from the inherent randomness
present in systems or processes. While the possible outcomes of such processes might be
known, this type of uncertainty lies in the unpredictability of which specific outcome will
occur at a given instance. Furthermore, aleatory uncertainty is characterised by its irreducibility, meaning that it cannot be reduced to a scalar regardless of additional information or data
acquisition. For instance, the variability in material properties like tensile strength or fatigue
life among identical samples illustrates aleatory uncertainty. Similarly, the unpredictability
of natural events such as radioactive decay is also a manifestation of this type of uncertainty.
Epistemic uncertainty, on the other hand, arises from incomplete or insufficient knowledge
about a system or process. In contrast to aleatory uncertainty, it can be reduced through
further research, data collection, or improved understanding about the processes involved. An
example of epistemic uncertainty can be found in the development of new technologies, such
as in nuclear fusion research, where uncertainties in plasma physics or blanket performance
may decrease as theoretical and experimental understanding advances.
The primary framework for mathematically describing uncertainty is probability theory,
which is grounded in Kolmogorov’s axioms. Let Ω be a non-empty set, over which F is an
algebra. Let P : F → R. Then P is a probability function and (Ω,F,P) is a probability space
if the following axioms are satisfied,
P(A) ≥ 0 , (1.12)
P(Ω) = 1 , (1.13)
P(A∪B) = P(A) +P(B), if A∩B = /0 , (1.14)
for every A and B in F. These axioms are called non-negativity, normalisation, and finite
additivity, respectively.
It is generally agreed that probability theory is the correct framework to handle aleatory
uncertainties [1], as these must be additive due to their relation to frequencies [57]. However,
in cases of epistemic uncertainty, these axioms can be overly conclusive in representing an
individual’s state of knowledge. For example, considering a scenario of coin flipping where
it is unknown whether the coin is fair or biased. According to the principle of insufficient
reason, a rational agent should assign equal probabilities to the outcomes when there is not

1.4 Epistemic uncertainty 19
basis to discriminate one over the other [58].9 Given the axioms of probability, specifically
finite additivity and normalisation, the sum of probabilities for all possible outcomes (heads
and tails) must equal 1. Therefore, by the principle of insufficient reason, P(heads) = P(tails).
This leads to P(heads) = P(tails) = 1
2
, implying equal likelihood for heads and tails. This
probability assignment parallels that for a known fair coin.10 Probability theory requires
assigning precise probability values even when evidence about the system is lacking. In cases
where judgement about a system’s properties should be suspended due missing information
(as with the unknown bias of the coin), probability theory still demands definitive probability
assignments. This can lead to counter-intuitive scenarios, where a system about which there
is lack of knowledge is modelled mathematically the same as a system perfectly known. This
example illustrates a critical aspect of probability theory in handling uncertainty, particularly
in distinguishing between aleatory and epistemic uncertainties. Probability theory cannot
distinguish between them, it conflates data and belief, and that difference is essential when
assessing models and propagating uncertainty through them [61].
There are, of course, middle grounds in which both aleatory and epistemic uncertainty
appear simultaneously. For example, it could be the case where the measurement of a
quantity was performed with an instrument of limited precision. The distribution function
resulting from the samples gathered by this instrument would be imprecisely defined, even
with an unlimited amount of data, since each sample would carry a certain level of inaccuracy.
Sometimes, the distribution function family of a variable is known, but its parameters,
such as mean or variance, remain uncertain due to the lack of data. These situations are
not uncommon in engineering [62], and call for mathematical methods that can describe
separately both aleatory and epistemic uncertainty at the same time [2]. The difficulty lies
in developing quantitative methods that avoid excluding crucial information and including
unwarranted assumptions. Failure to maintain this balance can result in computational
assessments that may deviate significantly from reality, potentially leading to drastic outcomes
in decision-making. The field of imprecise probabilities [63–65] attempts to develop such
mathematical methods. Some theories developed under the umbrella of imprecise probability
are evidence theory (also called Dempster–Shafer theory) [66, 67], possibility theory [68],
fuzzy set theory [69], and probability bounds analysis [70, 71].
In probability theory, the sum of the probability of an event and the probability of its
complement must be one. This implies that the absence of evidence for an event must
