1.4 Epistemic uncertainty 21
Probability bounds analysis takes a different approach by unifying probability theory and
interval analysis with the objective to provide guaranteed bounds on functions of random
variables given only partial knowledge of the probability distributions and their dependencies. Its structures, called p-boxes, are sets of distributions bounded by a lower and upper
cumulative distribution function. A p-box F(x) must satisfy the following constraints [73],
1. F(x) ≤ F(x) ≤ F(x) ,
2. µ ∈ [µ,µ], σ
2 ∈ [σ
2
,σ
2
] ,
3. F ∈ F .
A random variable will be a member of a p-box if its cumulative distribution function
falls within the bounds of the p-box F(x) ∈ [F(x),F(x)] for all x, its moments are within the
mean µ and variance σ
2 of the p-box, and it belongs to a family of distribution functions
F considered by the p-box (e.g. uniform, normal, or any other distribution). In some
circumstances is not required to satisfy all three constraints; for example, a p-box can be
defined from the cumulative distribution function and moment bounds, without knowing
the distribution family. Or the moments could be bounded from the cumulative distribution
function bounds and vice versa [74].
P-boxes offer a versatile framework for uncertainty quantification. Figure 1.8 shows a
comparative visualisation of a p-box under different cases of epistemic uncertainty. When
abundant knowledge about a process is available, p-boxes can have narrow bounds, approximating or being equivalent to a probability distribution function. This scenario indicates
strong confidence in the process’s behaviour. Conversely, limited knowledge results in
wider p-box bounds, meaning reduced confidence in specifying the distribution. In cases of
extreme uncertainty, where only the range of outcomes is known and the process is otherwise
uncharacterised, a p-box effectively becomes an interval, reflecting the broadest uncertainty.
Interval methods are appropriate for describing epistemic uncertainty since these only
require recognising that uncertainty exists within specified bounds, without further assumptions. This contrasts with other mathematical frameworks for uncertainty quantification. For
instance, probability distributions necessitate the distribution function to be precisely defined,
where usually this is performed by selecting a specific family and parameters. The quality of
the assessment on the derived risk and selected decisions will depend on the quality of the
selected functions. Some might argue that given that since epistemic uncertainty inherently
stems from a lack of knowledge, a representational form that imposes the fewest assumptions
aligns best with the concept of uncertainty and provides a more honest portrayal of the
unknown. Furthermore, in engineering contexts intervals are particularly versatile. They

22 Introduction
(a) Aleatory uncertainty. (b) Mixed uncertainty. (c) Epistemic uncertainty.
Fig. 1.8 Comparative visualisation of uncertainty types: Figure 1.8a presents a normal
CDF with x ∼ N (0.5,0.1
2
) illustrating pure aleatory uncertainty. Figure 1.8b depicts
a probability box (p-box) to represent both aleatory and epistemic uncertainties, where
x ∼ N ([0.45,0.55],0.1
2
), and the shaded area describes the epistemic uncertainty due to
limited or imprecise knowledge on the mean. Figure 1.8c illustrates an extreme case of
epistemic uncertainty, where the only information is x ∈ [0.2,0.8].
can represent various uncertainties, including a range of potential values, estimations of
numerical errors, and measurement inaccuracies due to limited precision. This adaptability
makes them highly applicable in engineering design and analysis, where quantifying and
managing uncertainty is crucial.
Critiques of using intervals analysis in uncertainty quantification often centre around
its potential for generating non-informative outputs, particularly when the interval bounds
are excessively wide [43]. This broad range can be perceived as a limitation, as it might
not offer precise guidance for decision-making or further analysis [75]. However, this
characteristic can also be interpreted positively. Wide intervals reflect a high degree of
uncertainty, accurately conveying the limited state of knowledge or data. In such cases,
the wide bounds serve as a cautionary indicator, suggesting that proceeding with caution
or acquiring more information might be necessary before making definitive decisions or
conclusions. This aspect of intervals is not inherently negative; rather, it aligns with a prudent
approach to dealing with uncertainty in scenarios where information is limited.
Some authors argue that probability theory can handle both aleatory and epistemic
uncertainty successfully, and there is no need for alternative theories [76, 77]. A premise
that this thesis holds is that in engineering assessments, either uncertainty quantification,
design optimisation, or risk analysis, knowing the source of the output uncertainties can
help to make better decisions. That is not possible when the uncertainties are described
with mathematical tools that conflate aleatory and epistemic uncertainty. Deciphering what
comes from inherent stochasticity and what comes from lack of knowledge is not possible
with probability theory for the reasons explained in this section. With respect to the former,
perhaps there is not much the analyst can do. But for the latter, it would be possible to design

1.4 Epistemic uncertainty 23
further experiments to reduce such uncertainty, or simply to say that given what is known it
is not possible to make an informed decision.



Chapter 2
Fusion Power Plants
The previous chapter introduced the phenomenon of nuclear fusion and presented a brief
history of nuclear fusion research. While different approaches for harnessing fusion power
coexist nowadays, this thesis focuses on tokamaks, a type of magnetic confinement fusion
(MCF) reactor. First, this chapter will attempt to introduce the basics of magnetic confinement
and the European DEMOnstration fusion power plant (DEMO). Then, some challenges
regarding DEMO will be identified and discussed: uncertainty quantification of DEMO
design, neutronics optimisation under uncertainty, and system reliability analysis. The
methodologies developed in this thesis attempt to tackle these three issues separately.
2.1 Magnetic confinement fusion
Fusion cross-sections describe the fusion reaction probability as a function of the colliding
energies of the nuclei (see Figure 1.2). Colliding particles often have a distribution of
energies, and it is useful to perform an average over the distributions of the product of the
cross-section σ and energy v, called reactivity. The fusion reaction rate (i.e. fusion reactions
per volume per time) can be calculated as
R = n1n2⟨σv⟩ , (2.1)
where n1 and n2 are the number densities of the colliding species and ⟨σv⟩ is the reactivity.
Thus, two ingredients are required for producing fusion reactions: energy and density.
Fortunately, it is not strictly necessary to run energies that maximise the fusion reaction
probability in order to achieve reactor-relevant conditions. In fact, Sun’s core temperature

26 Fusion Power Plants
is an order of magnitude from the peak of the relevant cross-sections,1 but the lack of
temperature is compensated with extraordinarily large number densities. Unfortunately, those
number densities are unattainable in tokamaks, requiring another ingredient: time.
At the temperatures required to achieve nuclear fusion, the fuel isotopes are fully ionised.
As a result, the electrons neutralise the electrostatic charge of the ions, and the fuel becomes
a quasineutral2 gas called plasma. In plasmas, electrons and ions coexist separately, and the
presence of these charged particles makes the plasma electrically conductive. Tokamaks take
advantage of this fact by confining the plasma particles in helical magnetic fields; that is how
confinement time adds to the recipe.
Figure 2.1 is a schematic representation of a tokamak reactor. The main magnetic fields
responsible for confining the plasma are the toroidal and poloidal magnetic fields. The
toroidal magnetic field is generated by the external coils that strap the plasma around the
torus. On the other hand, the poloidal magnetic field is generated by the current in the plasma
itself. The plasma current is driven by a toroidal electric field induced by the central solenoid
through a flux change (as a transformer action). The combination of the toroidal and poloidal
magnetic fields give rise to magnetic field lines with helical trajectory around the torus, which
prevent charged particles from escaping the confinement to some extent. Lastly, an external
vertical field generated by outer coils shapes the plasma and fixes the position of the plasma
current in the reactor.
When magnetically confined, charged particles travel in a closed loop a distance a million
times the dimensions of the vessel [78]. These particles are subject to a Lorentz force
F = q(E+v×B) , (2.2)
where q is the particle charge, E the electric field, v the velocity, and B the magnetic field.
This force constraints particle motion perpendicular to B, thus confining heat and particles
within the magnetic field lines. In addition to the Lorentz force, Maxwell’s equations are
also required to almost completely describe the dynamics of each charged particle:
∇·E =
ρ
ε0
(2.3)
∇·B = 0 (2.4)
1The Sun’s core reaches temperatures up to 15 million degrees Celsius, or 1.3 keV. The deuterium-tritium
fusion cross-section peaks at 70 keV.
2The prefix quasi- comes from the fact that, while charge density in plasmas is approximately zero at
macroscopic scale in tokamaks, the net electrical charge of any sufficiently small region is not zero. The length
above which plasmas can be assumed quasineutral is called Debye length (λD), which in tokamak plasmas is
about 10−4 m.

2.1 Magnetic confinement fusion 27
