
, (2.6)
where ρ is the charge density, ε0 and µ0 the vacuum permittivity and permeability, and J the
electric current density. While magnetic confinement may appear simple, particles interact
simultaneously with many others, leading to complex and collective phenomena, such as
turbulence. Turbulence has a significant impact on the confinement performance and some
heat and particles may inevitably escape from it [79]. Furthermore, plasma drifts, such as
E×B drift, grad-B drift, and curvature drift, arise due to the presence of electric and magnetic
fields and their gradients, as well as the curvature of the magnetic field lines. These drifts
can affect the plasma confinement and its stability causing asymmetries in plasma density
and temperature, contributing to cross-field transport and potentially leading to energy losses
and impacting the confinement performance [80]. There are other processes through which
energy is lost in tokamak plasmas, including cyclotron radiation due to particle motion in
magnetic fields, line radiation due to impurities in the plasma, Bremsstrahlung radiation due
to Coulomb collisions, and thermal conduction to the walls [81]. Therefore, to attain power
plant conditions in a tokamak reactor, the fusion power produced within the plasma must
exceed the power lost from the plasma.
Assuming an equal number of electrons and ions, the total energy in the plasma is
