2.1 Magnetic confinement fusion 29
This inequality is often referred to as the Lawson criterion3[22], and points out the requirements to achieve ignition, that is, when the nuclear fusion reactions in the plasma become
self-sustaining. Achieving ignition in magnetic confinement fusion marks a crucial point,
as only the energy needed to contain the plasma would be necessary (e.g. in the magnets,
cooling system, and other essential systems), eliminating the need for additional heating
power.
The fusion triple product, introduced in Chapter 1, can be derived from the Lawson
criterion, noting that the reactivity, ⟨σv⟩, is itself a function of temperature. A reasonable
approximation for the D-T reactivity in the 10−20keV range is
⟨σv⟩ ≈ 1.1×10−24T
