which provides an overall idea of the number density n, temperature T, and confinement time
τE conditions required to achieve ignition. Another indicator of progress toward achieving
reactor-like conditions is the fusion energy gain factor, denoted as Q, which represents the
ratio of the fusion power generated to the input heating power,
Q =
Pf
PH
. (2.15)
A point of interest for the fusion gain factor lies in the concept of break-even, which occurs
when the fusion power matches the supplied heating power, meaning Q = 1. Current best
gain factor in magnetic confinement dates back to 1997, when JET achieved Q = 0.64 [20].
One of ITER’s objectives is to produce 500 MW of fusion power using 50 MW of heating
power, proving conditions well beyond the break-even point with Q = 10. By achieving
this objective, ITER would establish the scientific groundwork for confining and controlling
ignited plasmas. Nonetheless, although achieving the successful control of ignited plasmas is
a pivotal milestone on the path to fusion power plants, it is important to note that ITER alone
will not be adequate to demonstrate the fundamental concept of a fusion power plant. That
will be the objective of the DEMOnstration fusion power plant (DEMO), of which this thesis
is focused.
3Lawson found that the product of density and energy confinement time were critical for a successful fusion
reactor. However, in its original paper the alpha particle heating was neglected, resulting in a more restrictive
criterion.

30 Fusion Power Plants
2.2 The European DEMO
While ITER will attempt to prove the control of break-even conditions in ignited plasmas,
a fusion power plant will require additional milestones, such as fuel self-sufficiency, high
availability, and production of electricity. In the European Roadmap to fusion energy [5], the
DEMOnstration fusion power plant (DEMO)4 will attempt to achieve these breakthroughs
with support of ITER and the International Fusion Materials Irradiation Facility – DEMO
Oriented NEutron Source (IFMIF-DONES) [82]. The European DEMO high level goals can
be described as
• self-sufficient fuel cycle, not requiring external sources of tritium,
• reliable operation and reasonable availability, with a pulse length around 2 hours,5
• favourable safety and environmental properties,
• net electricity production, in the range of 500 MW, and
• extrapolability to a fusion power plant.
Figure 2.2 shows an schematic diagram of the 2018 European DEMO. Most of the
in-vessel components in the ITER reactor will not apply to DEMO because they operate in
significantly distinct nuclear environments.6 Therefore, a significant number of technology
and physics integration challenges must be solved before a DEMO plant concept selection
is done. For example, DEMO will be fuelled with a deuterium-tritium mixture, which is
not naturally available. Thus, tritium will have to be produced through lithium-containing
materials deployed in the breeding blanket, which capture and breed tritium from the fusion
neutrons. Simultaneously, the first wall has to withstand the extreme conditions of plasma
operation, including high heat and neutron fluxes, while ensuring minimal material erosion
and plasma contamination. Furthermore, the first wall and breeding blanket are required to
transport the thermal power through the coolant to a power conversion system to produce
electricity. Consequently, designing a successful blanket, coolant, and first wall are crucial
not only for establishing tritium self-sufficiency for DEMO, but also to ensure a favourable
heat conversion to generate net electricity. The two main blanket technologies envisaged
4
In the literature, any fusion reactor with these objectives is a DEMO-class device. Therefore, one may
read about the Japanese DEMO, Korean DEMO, etc. In this thesis, however, DEMO will always refer to the
European DEMO (also called EU-DEMO).
5Due to the transformer action required to generate the poloidal field, tokamaks generally have to operate in
a pulsed regime. There are, however, concepts that envisage steady-state operating tokamaks.
6For example, DEMO will operate under higher neutron fluence.

2.2 The European DEMO 31
for the European DEMO are the helium-cooled pebble bed (HCPB) and the water-cooled
lithium lead (WCLL) [83].
Fig. 2.2 Radial build of the 2018 European DEMO baseline design.
Efficient plasma heating and current drive systems are also required to achieve long pulses.
There are three distinct phases during a plasma pulse: ramp-up, flat top, and ramp-down.
In the ramp-up, heat and current are steadily increased until plasma breakdown is achieved.
Ramp-up is followed by the flat-top phase, where the plasma current is kept constant.7 Lastly,
in the ramp-down phase heat and current have to be reduced carefully before termination in
order to prevent instabilities and disruptions. The role of heating and current drive systems is
to provide heating and core temperature control, and plasma current stabilisation during the
ramp phases. In tokamaks, plasma current is initially driven inductively by the transformer
action of the central solenoid coil. At the start of a pulse the plasma is not completely ionised
and causes resistive losses, so in order to maintain the plasma current is necessary to increase
the field in the coils to overcome the resistive losses, until the limiting field is reached. Then,
the plasma is shut down and the process is repeated. Therefore, to achieve long pulses
extending the flat-top phase, it is necessary to find a way to drive the plasma current without
relying on the central solenoid (i.e. non-inductively). This can be done with tangentially
injected neutral beams, which inject particles (hydrogen or deuterium) in the plasma at an
7
In pulsed reactors, the current in the central solenoid is slowly increased during the flat-top phase to drive
some plasma current.

32 Fusion Power Plants
angle, imparting toroidal momentum to the plasma and creating a toroidal current. Other
current drive methods require radio-frequency or microwave injection in the plasma.
Heating and current drive systems often share similar underlying principles, and sometimes the same technique can produce both heat and current. For example, the resistive losses
that appear in the plasma when the current is driven inductively contribute to raising the
temperature of the plasma itself. Likewise, the particles injected via neutral beam injection
impart their kinetic energy to the plasma via Coulomb scattering, providing heat and density.
Finding an appropriate mix of heating and current drive systems is critical for the success
of DEMO, and constitute challenging task due to the constrained space in the reactor, the
heat and radiation conditions the components must withstand, and the efficiencies of these
systems to maintain the reactor’s power balance. The current baseline assumes electron
cyclotron heating, with ion cyclotron waves and neutral beams being also developed in the
frame of a risk mitigation approach [84].
Another in-vessel component that is a major design driver for DEMO is the divertor,
depicted in Figure 2.3.
Fig. 2.3 Poloidal view of DEMO with a single null divertor design, showing plasma regions
and boundary surfaces critical for plasma-material interactions [85].

2.2 The European DEMO 33
The divertor is responsible for diverting the exhaust heat, dust particles, and helium ashes
from the plasma, and must be capable of effectively managing the power exhaust while
enduring high heat loads. During some transient events, such as Edge Localised Modes8, heat
loads on plasma facing components at ITER will be in the order of 10 MWm−2[86]. DEMO
will operate with longer pulses than ITER, requiring development of advanced materials
and cooling strategies capable of withstanding these conditions for longer periods without
maintenance. The European DEMO baseline builds a lower single-null divertor, which is
an ITER-like technology present in many successful reactors [87]. However, alternative
configurations are being investigated since there is a risk that the baseline configuration
cannot meet the plasma facing components technology limits while achieving the plasma
performance requirements of DEMO [88].
DEMO must also prioritise designs that minimise neutron activation, prevent the release
of radioactive substances, and reduce the long-lived radioactive waste to a minimum [89].
Employing low activation materials within the reactor’s structure is critical to ensuring that
components remain as radiation-safe as possible, even after prolonged operation [85]. At the
same time, these materials must be able to sustain high energy neutron fluences and thermal
and mechanical loads, without severe degradation of their mechanical and physical properties,
such as reduced activation ferritic/martensitic (RAFM) steels [90]. Carbides and some metals
are suitable for this role. Metallic materials show lower tritium diffusion and retention than
carbides, allowing reactor operations for longer periods without requiring tritium removal
procedures [91, 92]. Nevertheless, metals are more susceptible to erosion, which can result in
the transfer of impurities to the plasma and the degradation of protection of the metal. This,
in turn, has the potential to induce radiation cooling at the plasma’s edge and exacerbate
further instabilities, impacting the reactor’s performance. Currently, some of the materials
considered for DEMO are Eurofer97 (a reduced activation ferritic/martensitic steel alloy)
as structural material, tungsten for the plasma facing components, and CuCrZr for actively
cooled plasma facing components. Irradiation campaigns are carried out to validate and
qualify these materials and their development, but current data is based on heavy ion collision
or fission neutron irradiation. These conditions imply large uncertainties when extrapolating
the existing data to fusion scenarios. IFMIF-DONES will play a key role in minimising these
uncertainties, enabling material testing under DEMO-like neutron conditions [82, 93].
A high-performance plasma scenario with controllable instabilities and transport processes must also be developed for DEMO, as any sudden loss in the magnetic confinement can
8Edge Localised Modes (ELMs) are violent energy releases, usually caused by rapidly growing instabilities,
producing strong forces on the vessel walls. In the European DEMO, these events can deposit about 10 MJ in
one millisecond on the divertor plate. A complete physics explanation of how ELMs are originated is yet to be
established.

34 Fusion Power Plants
release large amounts of power to the in-vessel components [94]. However, high-performance
plasmas near the operational limits can suffer ELMs and disruptions, considered one of the
major concerns in tokamak plasmas [4]. Therefore, DEMO plasma scenarios should perform
without any transient event, employing ELM mitigation and suppression methods, or plasma
regimes that are naturally ELM-free [95]. Lastly, while DEMO does not require to be designed optimally concerning electricity cost or physics performance, it must show a potential
for its expansion into a fusion power plant [96].
Figure 2.4 depicts the European strategy for DEMO, with the different facilities and
their objectives in parallel. As the roadmap is event driven primarily depending on ITER’s
schedule, its timeline is not fixed. However, it can be seen that the final DEMO design
will not be fixed before ITER starts its operating phase, and the construction of DEMO
will not start before ITER achieves full performance, phases targeted for 2025 and 2036
respectively [97].9 Therefore, the formal selection of the DEMO reactor design has not taken
place, and detailed operational requirements are still lacking.
Fig. 2.4 The EUROfusion roadmap.
The current strategy follows a staged approach, depicted in Figure 2.5. During the
pre-conceptual design phase the resources were employed on assessing the main technical
feasibility issues and their risk, and identifying possible DEMO concept candidates that
9On 3 July 2024, the ITER Organization announced a new schedule targeting full magnetic energy in 2036
and the deuterium-tritium operation phase in 2039 [98].

2.2 The European DEMO 35
could meet the requirements. Since a significant amount of the design integration issues are
common crosswise over different concepts, there is a family of proposed DEMO concepts
alongside the baseline design, which is the most developed and low-risk design that works as
the standard reference point for integrating the European programme [99]. The main idea of
this strategy is to progressively narrow down in the design choices to the most feasible and
attractive one, as the fusion technology is developed in parallel. Once the pre-conceptual
design phase is completed, the conceptual design phase takes place. The purpose of this
phase is to turn the initial concept into a fully integrated system design to allow detailed
evaluation and validation of its technical feasibility, licensing, safety, and costs, so that
no major changes will be required during the engineering design phase. At the end of the
conceptual design phase, a baseline concept should be advanced enough to manifest the
required feasibility to begin with the next engineering design phase.
Fig. 2.5 The European DEMO staged design approach [100].
The outcomes of the pre-conceptual design phase were addressed in a set of publications [101], of which some of the lessons learned were:
• There are significant integration challenges due to the complex interdependencies
that affect the design and performance of DEMO, which require robust and reliable
solutions.

36 Fusion Power Plants
• Important knowledge gaps and uncertainties in essential reactor technologies will
remain even after ITER operation, demanding additional research and development
efforts. Some examples are the tritium breeding blanket and the ELM-free plasma
regimes.
• DEMO design activities must be capable of dealing with uncertainties in physics and
technology.
• A robust, repeatable, and traceable decision-making should be established for the
DEMO design.
Engineering design methodologies for mature technologies deal with well-defined boundaries and requirements, and face few complex interfaces and interdependencies. However,
DEMO presents an entirely distinct engineering paradigm: it is characterised by a scarcity of
explicit and detailed requirements, a multitude of interfaces and interdependencies, and embodies high level of complexity and uncertainty [102]. The outcomes of the pre-conceptual
design phase made evident that creating methodologies to simplify the intricate dependencies within DEMO, identify robust and reliable solutions, and manage uncertainties in the
decision-making processes are of paramount significance for the achievement of DEMO
objectives.
The following sections of this chapter describe three distinct challenges identified in
DEMO design activities. First, on the use of systems codes to guide DEMO concept selection
with uncertainty quantification and sensitivity analysis. Second, on the impact of neutronics
uncertainties in DEMO breeder blanket designs. And lastly, on component reliability data
and system’s failure calculations. This thesis aims to present methodologies specifically
developed to address these challenges, which are explained in the following chapters.
2.3 Systems codes, uncertainty quantification, and sensitivity analysis
Systems codes are computational codes with the objective to provide an integrated framework
for a fusion power plant, combining key physics, engineering, and economic models. These
codes enable the simulation of a specific fusion power plant concept by defining a set of
design inputs and constraints, providing a means to evaluate the feasibility of different design
pathways. Furthermore, systems codes can be used to optimise key figures of merit for fusion
power plants. For example, when optimising for maximum net electric power, a systems code
can adjust various design parameters such as magnetic field strength, plasma density, and

2.3 Systems codes, uncertainty quantification, and sensitivity analysis 37
geometry to find the design that yields the highest output for the given constraints. Similarly, if
minimising the reactor size is the priority, the code can iterate over the possible combinations
of materials, technologies, and operational parameters to find the smallest feasible design that
satisfies the performance requirements. An example of a systems code is PROCESS (Power
Reactor Optimisation Code for Environmental and Safety Studies) [103, 104]. In the context
of the European strategy, PROCESS is employed in the early-stage design and strategic
planning of DEMO, allowing for a high-level assessment of a concept’s performance before
narrowing down its operational space with more detailed and sophisticated codes [105].
Through the integrated models of systems codes, it is possible to explore the multidimensional design space efficiently and to identify configurations that best meet the specified
objectives. However, systems codes rely on simplifications and assumptions that can introduce uncertainties into the simulation results. For example, the use of a 0-1D plasma model
described by scaling laws in systems codes like PROCESS introduces simplifications that
omit detailed spatial and temporal dynamics of the plasma behaviour. These simplifications
can have an impact on the prediction of fundamental design drivers, such as the bootstrap
current10, divertor protection requirements, or fusion power [106]. Another source of uncertainties lies on the models’ parameters. An example is the ITERH-98P scaling law [33],
which is employed in PROCESS to predict the energy confinement time as a function of
various parameters of the reactor and its operating conditions, defined as
τE = CIαIB
αBPαPnαnMαM RαRεαεκακ
, (2.16)
with I being the plasma current, B toroidal magnetic field at major radius, P loss power, n
line average density, κ plasma elongation, ε inverse aspect ratio, M average ion mass, and R
major radius (in s, MA, T, MW, 1019m−3, AMU, m). The non-dimensional coefficient C and
the exponents α are obtained with statistical regression techniques on the experimental data
contained in different databases, such as [107] or [108]. It has been found that the ITERH98P scaling law and its parameters can impact dramatically the size of the reactor [109].
In addition to the models and parameters uncertainties, DEMO requirements remain vague
and high-level [102], with the consequence of not being able to have a definite DEMO
configuration. Hence, addressing these uncertainties is critical in providing reliable and
robust design recommendations. This involves the development of uncertainty quantification
and sensitivity analysis methods to help understand the impact of input uncertainties on the
outputs of such models. These activities can help to improve the predictive capabilities of
10The bootstrap current arises within the plasma due to collisions between trapped and passing particles.
Maximising the bootstrap current is generally beneficial, as it reduces the reliance on an external current drive
system, thereby lowering the reactor’s operational costs.

38 Fusion Power Plants
systems codes, and their trustworthiness for decision-making in the engineering design of
fusion power plants.
Uncertainty quantification methods play a crucial role in the early stages of the design
process, offering a quantitative assessment of various concept choices. These methods are
instrumental in identifying high-impact areas in the chosen concept that require further
development to align with specified requirements, or that may present risks for finding a
successful design. Some previous work has been carried out in uncertainty quantification
with PROCESS; on the European DEMO [99, 110–113], on the China Fusion Engineering
Test Reactor (CFETR) [114], or the HELIcal Advanced Stellarator (HELIAS) [115].
The capability of systems codes to facilitate a quantitative comparison of various fusion
power plant concepts is a key aspect of their utility. As an example, the high confinement
mode (H-mode) has been the preferred plasma operation regime for fusion power plants
for decades due to its enhanced performance [116]. However, recent concerns about the
impact of type-I Edge Localised Modes (ELMs) associated with H-mode plasmas have led
to the proposal of new plasma operational scenarios [95, 117]. In this context, designing
methodologies and metrics for comparing different DEMO concepts becomes critical. These
methodologies must be capable of:
1. Evaluating how the current state of knowledge influences each DEMO concept.
2. Identifying the specific areas of knowledge that need refinement to ensure the success
for various DEMO concepts.
Developed within the framework of uncertainty quantification and sensitivity analysis, these
methodologies comprise two main components:
• Uncertainty quantification, which focuses on assessing and managing the uncertainties
inherent in each DEMO concept.
• Sensitivity analysis, involving two processes:
1. Determining how parameter uncertainties impact the reliability of outcomes for
each DEMO concept.
2. Identifying which parameters require further investment to reduce uncertainty,
thereby enhancing confidence in each concept.
In [112] it is argued that DEMO design parameters are imprecise due to lack of knowledge,
and for this reason when performing uncertainty quantification activities using fusion systems
codes, uncertainties have to be treated as epistemic and not aleatory. Using precise, fixed

2.4 Neutronics optimisation under uncertainty 39
values and distributions for some design parameters would not be a reliable option given
the lack of evidence about what will be reasonably achievable for the European DEMO.
For example, a fusion reactor can be designed to operate with a certain plasma elongation,
yet for the 2015 European H-mode DEMO it was found that the maximum acceptable
plasma elongations (at 95% flux surface) regarding vertical stability in the ramp-down and
flat top phases were 1.59 and 1.71 respectively [118]. Which value of plasma elongation
to use for the design of the device is an important question since there could exist better
vertical stability measures in the future, helping to raise the 1.59 lower limit, but at the
same time, it would be recommended to be conservative on the 1.71 upper limit, since
certain plasma instabilities can reduce the vertical stability. The answer to this question is,
therefore, subordinated to the technology and knowledge available at the time of design and
construction of DEMO [119, 120], and treating uncertainties in DEMO design parameters as
epistemic uncertainties helps to acknowledge this fact during DEMO design activities.
The methodology proposed in [112] is based on interval analysis. Intervals represent the
least amount of useful knowledge since only the bounds of the interval have to be assumed
for each uncertain parameter, hence being ideal for capturing epistemic uncertainty. Another
advantage of intervals is that, regardless of the possible differences in nature between design
parameters and uncertain parameters, these equally capture both design space and epistemic
uncertainty, making no difference on the modelling and computational analysis. Hence,
modelling design parameters employing intervals also helps to find the feasible design space,
as this methodology shows.
Chapter 3 elaborates the interval-based methodology to perform uncertainty quantification
and sensitivity analysis under the presence of epistemic uncertainty, as required in the DEMO
concept design and selection activities.
2.4 Neutronics optimisation under uncertainty
Deuterium-tritium fusion reactions produce high-energy neutrons that will escape from
the tokamak’s magnetic confinement as a consequence of their neutral charge, leading to
various detrimental effects and potentially compromising reactor performance. These effects
include nuclear heating, collision cascades, and transmutation [121]. Nevertheless, neutron
irradiation is essential for reactor operation, since neutrons are necessary for both tritium
production and electricity generation.
Nuclear heating occurs when a material is exposed to neutron bombardment. Part of the
kinetic energy of the neutrons is absorbed by the material, increasing its temperature. This
heating effect is detrimental where maintaining low temperatures is critical, and can lead to

40 Fusion Power Plants
thermal stresses and deformation of reactor components. Furthermore, to enhance reactor’s
efficiency, it is crucial that the neutrons deposit as much energy as possible in the blanket,
the reactor’s heat exchange part for electricity generation, rather than in other components.
Collision cascades are a sequence of atomic displacements that occur when energetic
neutrons collide with the atoms in reactor materials. These cascades result in significant
structural damage at the atomic level, manifesting as crystallographic defects, such as
dislocations and voids within the material. This degradation can compromise the mechanical
and physical properties of the materials, such as strength, ductility, and thermal conductivity.
To maintain plasma confinement, superconducting magnets must operate at extremely low
temperatures. These magnets can lose their superconducting properties if their temperature
exceeds a critical threshold. This phenomenon, known as quenching, can lead to a sudden
loss of magnetic field, disrupting the plasma confinement and potentially damaging the
reactor. Furthermore, superconducting magnets often employ materials such as niobium-tin
or REBCO [122], and collision cascades can lead to a degradation in their superconducting
properties and damage the coil insulation. The accumulation of crystallographic defects can
lower their critical current density, reducing the magnet’s ability to generate strong magnetic
fields [123]. In general, it has been observed that neutron irradiation has detrimental effects
on superconducting compounds [124]. Therefore, efficient radiation shielding and cooling
systems are crucial to protect the magnets from excessive nuclear heating and damage [125].
Another undesirable effect is neutron-induced transmutation, a process where neutron
absorption leads to changes in the elemental and isotopic composition of materials. This
transmutation can activate materials, making them radioactive and thereby generating hazardous waste. A critical requirement for the acceptance and commercial viability of fusion
power plants is that these should not generate long-lived radioactive waste of significant
concern. Hence, the materials activated in fusion reactors should decay to low-level waste
or less within a century after their removal [126].11 However, the presence of impurities in
materials like Eurofer97 could pose challenges in achieving these low activation levels [128].
Research is focused on innovative materials that exhibit minimal activation and have shorter
half-lives, such as high-entropy alloys [129], ensuring that any induced radioactivity decays
to safe levels within a reasonable timeframe post-reactor decommissioning.
On the other hand, neutrons are crucial for the successful operation of fusion power
plants. Deuterium, readily available in seawater, presents no significant long-term supply
11It is important to note that the regulation regarding radioactive waste does not necessarily apply internationally. In [127], the authors say: “The levels of 14C that make Eurofer97 problematic in UK law are not a
problem under French regulations, and conversely the levels of 94Nb which would be problematic for French
regulators would not be an issue under UK regulatory law. Additionally, the levels of both 14C and 94Nb do not
consitute the need for geologic disposal in Japan at all”.

2.4 Neutronics optimisation under uncertainty 41
challenges. In contrast, tritium, a radioactive isotope with a half-life of approximately 12.3
years, is practically non-existent in nature on Earth. Although tritium can be produced in
CANDU and similar heavy water fission reactors, this method is unlikely to be sufficient to
fulfil the tritium inventory demands for fusion applications [130]. Therefore, future fusion
power plants will have to be able to produce their own tritium supply to be self-sufficient.
Current design strategies aim to produce tritium through low-energy fission reactions in
lithium, as in
6Li+n → 3H+4He , (2.17)
7Li+n → 3H+4He+n . (2.18)
To increase the amount of tritium produced per fusion-born neutron, lithium is often
combined with elements such as beryllium or lead due to their neutron multiplication
properties. These elements can produce more neutrons in reactions such as
9Be+n → 24He+2n , (2.19)
204Pb+n → 203Pb+2n . (2.20)
The strategy is to have a blanket surrounding the plasma with lithium and a neutron
multiplier in it, so neutrons interact with lithium and produce tritium. Furthermore, neutrons
carry about 80% of the fusion energy that will need to be converted into heat and electricity.
Thus, the kinetic energy of the fusion-born neutrons should be converted into thermal energy
and extracted by the coolant contained in the blanket to facilitate the production of electricity.
The tritium breeding blanket is situated between the first wall and the vacuum vessel of
a fusion reactor, as illustrated in Figure 2.6 (see also Figure 2.2). Its main objective is to
produce tritium required to fuel the reactor, but it is also instrumental in extracting heat from
the kinetic energy of incident neutrons and providing shielding for reactor components and
external structures from neutron irradiation [131]. The blanket is, therefore, an indispensable
component of a fusion power plant.
However, to date, a fully operational breeding blanket has not yet been constructed or
tested. This absence of experimental data introduces severe epistemic uncertainties in its
design and performance [133]. ITER is aiming to address this gap by incorporating test
blanket modules, designed to trial various aspects of this technology [134]. While ITER
will not fully deploy breeding blanket technology, these modules represent a significant step
towards understanding breeding blanket designs in an integrated fusion environment [131].

42 Fusion Power Plants
Fig. 2.6 Schematic radial build of a fusion reactor [132].
Due to the lack of experimental data of breeding blanket performance, neutronics simulations are essential in the design and evaluation of blanket concepts for fusion power plants.
However, these simulations are not without their challenges, primarily arising from various
uncertainties and necessary assumptions [135]. These include the omission of material
impurities, the homogenisation of complex geometries, and the discretisation of the spatial
domain. Furthermore, the simulations often assume temporal invariance in both plasma
neutron source and material composition. Additionally, the reliability of these simulations
is dependent on the accuracy of nuclear data, which is subject to significant uncertainties
for fusion applications [136]. These factors collectively impact the precision of neutronics
simulations, and require of careful consideration and mitigation strategies. Chapter 4 further
develops on the implications of these assumptions and uncertainties, and presents a novel
methodology to deal with nuclear data uncertainty in the context of breeder blanket design
optimisation.
2.5 Component reliability and system’s failure
Reliable operation, reasonable availability, and favourable safety and environmental properties are some of the requirements of DEMO. These requirements are intimately tied to the
failure modes and rates of the power plant systems and its components. Hence, risk analyses,

2.5 Component reliability and system’s failure 43
such as fault-tree analysis, are necessary to understand what are the most likely routes of
failure in a system, show compliance with the reliability requirements, or design monitoring
strategies.
Fault trees are graphical models that represent the combinations of failures or events
that could lead to the top event, which is the event to prevent or mitigate. The diagrams
are constructed using a series of logic gates (e.g. AND and OR) and primary or initiating
events (e.g. pipe breaks, valve malfunctions, etc.). An example of a fault tree is shown in
Figure 1.7. With fault trees it is possible to express the probability of the top event as a
function of gates of primary events. However, since the primary and initiating events are not
defined in terms of other events, these require the specification of their probability. Another
fundamental requirement of fault-tree analysis is the identification of accident scenarios or
event sequences leading to the top event [137], but this remains beyond the scope of this
thesis.
There are different methods to estimate component failure rates, and these depend on
the component’s mission (demand based or time based) and their type (degradable, nondegradable, repairable, etc.) [50]. For example, for demand based components the probability
of failure can be calculated as
pf =
r
n
, (2.21)
where r is the failure count and n the total number of demands. Demand failures are often
modelled with the binomial distribution, being
F(r,n, pf) = 
