Fig. 3.1 Mixture of Clayton, Gumbel, and Student’s t copula.
Thus, despite being an inexpensive method, scatterplot examination is a qualitative
method that does not appear to be reliable when there is imprecision or uncertainty about the
relationships in the data.

3.2 Limitations of current sensitivity analysis methods under epistemic uncertainty 51
Other well known measures employed in sensitivity analysis are correlation coefficients,
such as Pearson’s or Spearman’s. The Pearson’s correlation coefficient is defined as
ρX,Y =
cov(X,Y)
σXσY
, (3.1)
where X,Y are a pair of random variables, cov is their covariance, and σ the standard
deviation. Usually, X is an input parameter and Y the model output. The Spearman’s
correlation is equal to the Pearson’s correlation between the rank values of X and Y.
The potential limitations of these correlation coefficients are that Pearson’s measures only
linear dependence, whilst Spearman’s relaxes this limitation by measuring monotonic dependence. Under imprecise settings it is often unknown whether the model under investigation
presents linear or monotonic dependence with the inputs, thus it can be difficult to justify
the use of these measures. In fact, encountering non-linear and non-monotonic models is a
common occurrence. Also, both Pearson’s and Spearman’s can fail to detect dependence in
complex dependence structures, as in the following function [165]:
Y = 0.2X1 −5X2 +10X21X3≥0,with X1,X2,X3 ∼ U (−1,1) . (3.2)
Figure 3.2 shows the evaluation of Equation (3.2) with 10000 samples. Note how both
Pearson’s and Spearman’s return ∼ 0 correlation, despite there is a clear dependence between
X2 and Y.
