Furthermore, when there is lack of data, the difference between Pearson’s and Spearman’s
can be striking. For example, in Figure 3.3 the two methods report contradicting measures.

52 Sensitivity Analysis
According to Pearson’s, the variables have positive correlation, but Spearman’s reports
negative correlation.3 Hence, correlation measures such as Pearson’s or Spearman’s are not
well suited for settings under epistemic uncertainty, complex dependence structures, or lack
of data.
