Fig. 3.3 Scatterplot of x = [−3,−2.5,0.13,0.5] and y = [−0.5,−0.75,2.5,−1.0]. Pearson’s and
Spearman’s correlation coefficients return contradicting results, 0.4 and -0.4 respectively.
Derivative-based methods rely on the derivatives of the output with respect to its inputs.
These methods measure how a small change in an input parameter affects the output. The
derivative can be quantified analytically, if the model is simple enough, or numerically using
techniques such as finite difference. A critical step in derivative-based sensitivity analysis
is the selection of a specific point in the input parameter space, which can significantly
influence the results and interpretations. Often, there is no clear consensus on which point
or set of points should be selected for sensitivity analysis. This choice may depend on the
objectives of the analysis, prior knowledge about the system, or the range of parameter values
of interest. This ambiguity can lead to different conclusions based on where the analysis
is centred. Furthermore, for models with a large input space selecting a representative
point becomes challenging, which may not capture the variability and potential interaction
among parameters across the entire space. This is a well-known issue in local sensitivity
analysis [166]).
Another well known method for sensitivity analysis are Sobol’ indices [40]. This is
a variance-based method that decomposes the output variance and assigns it to the inputs
3This is an extreme case of limited data, where a rigorous analyst would not employ correlation coefficients.
However, the example shows that when there is not enough data, Pearson’s and Spearman’s correlation
coefficients can return opposite results.

3.2 Limitations of current sensitivity analysis methods under epistemic uncertainty 53
variance (i.e. measures how much of the output variance can be apportioned to the inputs
variance). The first-order sensitivity index, which accounts for the main effect contribution
of each input (i.e. the effect of varying Xi alone), is calculated as:
Si =
V[E(Y|Xi)]
V(Y)
, (3.3)
where V[E(Y|Xi)] is the variance of the conditional expected value of the output varying Xi,
and V(Y) is the variance of the output. It is also possible to account for interaction effects
in Sobol’ indices (i.e. the effect of varying several inputs simultaneously), which can be
calculated with the total order indices as:
STi =
E[V(Y|X∼i)]
V(Y)
= 1−
V[E(Y|X∼i)]
V(Y)
, (3.4)
where X∼i refers to all inputs except i. Note that the total order also accounts for the
main effects, thus the difference between the total order and the first-order indicates the
existence or nonexistence of interaction effects. A potential drawback of variance-based
methods such as Sobol’ indices is that they require the definition of the input probability
distribution functions (e.g. x1 ∼ N (0,1), x2 ∼ U (−1,1) in y = f(x1, x2)). It is often
the case that under epistemic uncertainty or imprecision, assuming that some variable
follows certain probability distribution function with precise parameters can be difficult
to justify. Also, since the variance of the output will depend on the input distributions,
the sensitivity indices will be affected by these assumptions. For example, Sobol’ indices
can return contradictory ranks for the inputs when the knowledge about their distributions
is imprecise [147]. Furthermore, sensitivity analysis can be employed not only to rank
parameters by importance, but also to understand how to maximise the output uncertainty
reduction by reducing uncertainty about the input parameters. In the case of variance-based
methods, the output uncertainty reduction is an average reduction of its variance when the
variance of certain input is reduced by some amount [167]. This process can be complicated
to decipher, and in some circumstances reducing the variance of a variable by some precise
amount is simply not possible. Additionally, the variance employed in these settings is
not the descriptive variance from a set of observations, but the variance of a theoretical
probability distribution. Following our distinction between aleatory and epistemic uncertainty,
probability distributions successfully capture stochasticity or irreducible uncertainty. Thus,
the ‘reduction of the variance’ is an oxymoron, since the variance is already describing an
uncertainty that cannot be reduced.

54 Sensitivity Analysis
Some studies suggest that metamodels (such as polynomial chaos expansions or gaussian
processes) can be employed in high-dimensional problems to reduce the computational
cost ([168] and references therein). Polynomial chaos expansion requires the definition
of precise probability distributions, as this method approximates them with polynomials.
It is known that imprecision in probability distributions limit the utility of polynomial
chaos expansions [169]. Other metamodels, such as gaussian processes, are often based on
smoothness and assumptions that may cause overfitting (e.g. linearity, monotonicity, etc.).
The list of methods mentioned in this section is not exhaustive, but covers the main
methods that are employed in the literature of sensitivity analysis: scatterplots, correlation
coefficients, partial derivatives, Sobol’ indices, and metamodels. These methods are not
suitable for performing sensitivity analysis under epistemic uncertainty or imprecision, as
it has been illustrated, for different reasons. Other methods not discussed herein, whether
derivative-based, distribution-based, or regression-based, also encounter limitations similar
to those highlighted, including the necessity for defining precise probability distributions or
input space points, and making model assumptions.
3.3 Properties of an ideal sensitivity analysis method
Different sensitivity methods consider important different things. For example, Sobol’
indices measure function variances while derivative-based methods measure the variation of
the function derivative around certain points. Hence, there is not a best sensitivity analysis
method, since that choice is problem specific. The analyst must choose a method depending
on the model functions, parameter evidence, computational cost, audience, and similar
factors [170]. However, there are some properties that a sensitivity analysis method should
have in order to provide guarantees. The Rényi postulates for a dependence measure to be
appropriate, denoted by δ(x, y) where x is the model input and y the output, are [171]:
1. δ(x, y) is defined for any pair of random variables x and y, neither of them being
constant with probability 1.
2. δ(x, y) = δ(y, x).
3. 0 ≤ δ(x, y) ≤ 1.
4. δ(x, y) = 0 if and only if x and y are independent.
5. δ(x, y) = 1 if there is a strict dependence between x and y, i.e. x = g(y) or y = f(x)
where g and f are Borel-measurable functions.

3.3 Properties of an ideal sensitivity analysis method 55
6. If the Borel-measurable functions g and f map the real axis in a one-to-one way onto
itself, δ(f(x),g(y)) = δ(x, y).
7. If the joint distribution of x and y is normal, then δ(x, y) = |ρ(x, y)|, where ρ is the
Pearson’s correlation coefficient.
Postulate (1) is required for a measure to be well defined, and postulate (2) generalises the
symmetry property. Perhaps from an analyst perspective the most relevant postulates are (3),
(4), and (5). These bound the measure to the [0,1] interval, where 0 implies independence
and 1 implies strict dependence between X and Y. When these postulates hold, a null value of
the sensitivity measure can be taken as a guarantee that Y is independent of X. Furthermore,
when the sensitivity measure equals to 1, then it is guaranteed that there exists some strict
dependence that can be explained with a Borel-measurable function f : y = f(x). If these
postulates do not hold, then the analyst is limited to compare the degrees of dependence
between Y and the inputs (e.g. Y has greater dependence on X1 than on X2), and the
identification of independence or strict dependence can be misleading or not possible. Lastly,
postulate (6) is related to the property of invariance, and postulate (7) employs the bivariate
normal distribution and the Pearson’s correlation coefficient as the general case. Note that the
latter is only relevant if the uncertainties are described in the context of probability theory.
There are also some other properties that are desirable, in general, for a sensitivity analysis
method to be practical [172]. For instance, the sensitivity measure should incorporate the
effect of the scale and shape of the inputs. If the uncertainty of an input decreases, ceteris
paribus, its sensitivity index should decrease as its impact on the prediction of the output
becomes less relevant. Another property is that the method should be global rather than local.
Local methods calculate the effects on the model output varying by small amounts the inputs
around a nominal value. Also, it is often the case that this variation is performed one input
at a time; this is, varying one while keeping the others constant. Global methods, on the
other hand, do not restrict the analysis around nominal values, and do not necessarily keep
any input constant. Lastly, it should be model independent. Complex models or models of
which there is little evidence about their behaviour are usually the main target of sensitivity
analysis. Therefore, it seems reasonable for a sensitivity analysis method to rely as less as
possible on the linearity, monotonicity, additivity, or any kind of possible behaviour a model
can have. The previous section highlighted some consequences of applying methods that do
not comply with these requirements.
In addition to these specific and general requirements, there are some other properties
that would be advantageous to have. Sensitivity analysis is an activity usually performed in
conjunction with uncertainty quantification. A common uncertainty quantification procedure

56 Sensitivity Analysis
is performed via forward uncertainty propagation, in which samples of the uncertain inputs
are propagated through the computational model and the information about the uncertain
output is extracted. It would be desirable to be able to perform sensitivity analysis with the
exact same samples employed for uncertainty quantification, with no further simulations
required. Another typical objective of sensitivity analysis is uncertainty reduction. The goal
is to identify the inputs that yield the maximum output uncertainty reduction when their
uncertainty is reduced. This is useful in the context of R&D, where analysts have to identify
how to best distribute resources to reduce input uncertainty. Again, it would be desirable to
obtain the uncertainty reduction information without having to perform further simulations
or analyses, employing the same samples that were used for uncertainty quantification and
sensitivity analysis. Finally, in the context of epistemic uncertainty, a proper sensitivity
analysis method should be able to deal with imprecision in the characterisation of input
uncertainty, such as the pinching method with probability bounds analysis [173], or require
the minimum amount of assumptions regarding input uncertainty.
3.4 Interval-based global sensitivity analysis
With interval analysis it is possible to determine the bounds of the output of a function and
capture its imprecision. Expressing the uncertainty of the inputs and outputs in the form
of intervals has the advantage of requiring no assumptions about the uncertainty other than
the their existence between two bounds, making intervals suitable for modelling epistemic
uncertainty. Also, when there is functional dependence between the inputs and the outputs,
the examination of scatterplots can be an inexpensive method to investigate dependencies
with any kind of black-box model response (e.g. linear, nonmonotonic, etc.). The objective
of the interval-based global sensitivity analysis is to combine interval analysis and scatterplot
examination to transform the qualitative intuition of scatterplots into a quantitative measurement, without having to make assumptions about the uncertainty of the inputs and outputs or
the model response.
As an illustrative example, Figure 3.4 shows the evaluation of the function f(x1, x2) =
−4x1 +0x2 +ε with ε ∼ N (0, x1) with 1000 samples . From the scatterplot it can be seen
that y presents some linear dependence and heteroscedasticity with x1, while appears to be
independent of x2 (which is correct since x2 is multiplied by zero in f).
The proposed sensitivity index Si quantifies the relationship between the output y and the
i
th input parameter xi
. This index measures the area described by the scatterplot of y against
xi, and compares it with the area of the bounding rectangle defined by [y, y]×[xi, xi], where

3.4 Interval-based global sensitivity analysis 57
y, y are the minimum and maximum of y, and xi, xithe minimum and maximum of the i
th
input parameter.
The sensitivity index Siis calculated with the following formula:
Si = 1−
∑
N
n=1
(xi,n −xi,n)(yn −yn)
(xi −xi)(y−y)
, (3.5)
where N is the number of subintervals in the domain of xi used to calculate the index, xi,n
and xi,n are the upper and lower bounds of the n
th subinterval for xi
, yn and yn are the upper
and lower bounds of the n
th subinterval for y, (xi,n −xi,n) is the width of the nth subinterval
for xi, and (yn −yn) is the width of the n
th subinterval for y. The numerator sums the areas of
the rectangles defined by the subintervals, and the denominator is the area of the bounding
rectangle.
Figure 3.5 shows the calculation of the measured scatterplot area and bounding rectangle
area for the previous example, with 20 subintervals. The algorithm requires the input and
output data, and the number of subintervals to calculate the areas. The minimum and
maximum of the output are obtained for each subinterval, and the area of the rectangle
defined by these is calculated. The total scatterplot area is the sum of all the red rectangles’
areas. In this example, the sensitivity indices of x1 and x2 calculated with Equation (3.5)
are S1 = 0.790 and S2 = 0.197, indicating that x1 has a greater impact on y than x2, as was
previously guessed.
