58 Sensitivity Analysis
3.4.1 Computation
The sensitivity measure proposed can be computed with the same methods required for
interval analysis, with the only addition of Equation (3.5). The objective of interval analysis
is to compute the output interval of a function, given that its inputs are expressed in the
form of intervals. There are, at least, three different ways to obtain this output interval: via
sampling, optimisation, or with interval arithmetic.
The typical sampling procedure begins with the definition of uniform distributions for
each input, bounded in their respective interval as xi ∈ U (xi, xi), and then producing S
input samples ((x1, x2,..., xI)1,...,(x1, x2,..., xI)S) with a sampling algorithm. The algorithm
of choice is typically Latin Hypercube, since it stratifies uniformly each distribution and
guarantees a uniform coverage of the input intervals (maximising the exploration of the space
for a given number of inputs), but can be any (e.g. random sampling). Then, the function
y = f(x) is evaluated at each input sample, and the output interval y ∈ [y, y] is obtained by
extracting the minimum and maximum output found. Recall that to compute Equation (3.5)
it is required not only to find the global output interval [y, y], but also the output interval
[y, y]n in each subinterval n ∈ N. While the sampling method is surprisingly simple, and
usually a good initial approximation, it has some drawbacks. Its main drawback is that the
resulting output interval is an inner approximation of the actual output. This is an artifact
inherent of sampling methods, which inevitably will leave some region of the function’s
domain unsampled. Increasing S will make the resulting output interval converge to the
