3.4 Interval-based global sensitivity analysis 59
actual output, though. Another potential cause of imprecision in Equation (3.5) is the number
of subintervals employed to compute it. A lower number of subintervals will return a coarser
sensitivity measure, while a greater number will produce a finer extraction of [y, y]n. Note
that if the number of subintervals is exceedingly large, there could be some subintervals
with zero samples inside them, causing an error in the computation. Thus, the number of
samples and the number of subintervals should be balanced. In conclusion, the calculation of
the sensitivity index with sampling methods will be affected by the sampling algorithm of
choice, the number of samples, and the number of subintervals.
Interval analysis can also be defined as an optimisation problem, such as min/max f(x)
subject to xi ∈ [xi, xi]. Thus, an optimisation algorithm can be implemented to find f and
f , by minimising and maximising f(x). This method can return more rigorous intervals
compared to sampling, since the optimisation algorithm is designed with the goal of obtaining
the optima of the function. When the number of inputs is prohibitively large for sampling
methods due to the curse of dimensionality, optimisation methods can be advantageous.
However, Equation (3.5) requires not only the global minimum and maximum, but also the
minimum and maximum of each subinterval. This requires a total of 2 ∗N ∗ I optimisation
processes: two (minimisation and maximisation) for each subinterval n ∈ N and input
variable i ∈ I. The computational cost of this method can be very restrictive for objective
functions that are expensive to compute, and a surrogate model could be required. The peril
of employing a surrogate model is that it is an approximation of the actual objective function,
diluting the possible advantage of optimisation over sampling in finding the rigorous output
interval. Furthermore, there is a plethora of optimisation algorithms available, each of them
best suited for particular models or tasks. When there is little or no information about the
behavior of the model it can be very difficult to justify using one algorithm over another.
Thus, using optimisation methods to calculate Equation (3.5) should be well justified (e.g. if
sampling methods cannot guarantee an exhaustive search).
Lastly, interval analysis can be naturally performed with interval arithmetic. In interval
arithmetic, the four basic arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, and
division) are defined to work with intervals instead of precise numbers [162]. The main
drawback of interval arithmetic is that it is an intrusive method, since the models under
investigation have to be prepared beforehand to work with it. Furthermore, interval arithmetic
can suffer from a well known efficiency problem called dependency problem or wrapping
effect. This problem arises when a mathematical function includes the same input variable
repeated times, leading to a wider output interval [174]. It is a well known problem in the
interval arithmetic community, and several methods have been proposed to avoid it, such as
centered forms as in [162, 175], affine arithmetic [176], Taylor models [177], and so forth. A

60 Sensitivity Analysis
possible way to overcome this problem is to rewrite the mathematical functions to eliminate
or reduce the input repetition, but it is not always possible to do so. Another way to reduce
the impact of the repeated variables is to subintervalise the input variables, and compute
the function at each subinterval. The greater the number of subintervals, the lesser will be
the impact of the repeated variables on the output interval. To compute Equation (3.5) it is
required to perform subintervalisation; thus, it can be said that the dependency problem is
minimised.
The function from the previous example, f(x1, x2) = −4x1 +0x2 +ε with x1 ∈ [−5,5],
x2 ∈ [−5,5], has been used to illustrate the performance of the different methods in computing Equation (3.5), shown in Figure 3.6a for sampling, Figure 3.6b for optimisation, and
Figure 3.6c for interval arithmetic. These figures show the calculated S1 (dashed line) and S2
(solid line) for a different number of subintervals. In the case of sampling (Figure 3.6a), Latin
Hypercube was employed to produce 500, 1000, 2000, 5000, 10000, and 20000 samples. It
is possible to see how Si converges faster when a greater number of samples is employed.
For optimisation (Figure 3.6b), SciPy’s differential evolution was chosen as the optimisation algorithm [178] for three reasons: it is a global optimisation algorithm, evolutionary
algorithms can deal with noisy functions successfully, and does not employ derivatives
(unless known, it should not be assumed that the functions in the model under study are
differentiable). In any case, the selection of the optimisation algorithm is problem specific,
and goes beyond the scope of this chapter. It is important to consider that sometimes the
computational cost of optimisation is significantly greater than sampling. Thus, optimisation methods are not advisable for performing an initial sensitivity analysis with screening
purposes when models are too complex or computationally demanding.
The sensitivity analysis performed with interval arithmetic (Figure 3.6c) is capable of
returning the actual Si, as in the case of x2 returned 0 for every subinterval. Note how the index
for x1 is not 1, since there is some variation of the output that is not caused by x1. Therefore,
if accuracy is important, it should be the preferable method over sampling and optimisaton.
Also, in terms of computational time interval arithmetic is comparable to sampling, thus
being advantageous over optimisation. This is true if the functions have already been adapted
to operate with intervals; otherwise there would be some code development time cost to take
into account.
Note how for the same number of subintervals, interval arithmetic returns the smallest Si
while sampling returns the largest Si, and optimisation returns a value between them. This
is because the subintervals calculated with interval arithmetic will always be of greater or
equal width than those obtained with sampling or optimisation. Also, in general, subintervals

3.4 Interval-based global sensitivity analysis 61
obtained via optimisation tend to be wider than those obtained via sampling. This can be
visualised in Figure 3.7, which was generated with 20 subintervals.
(a) Convergence of S1 (dashed line) and S2 for
2, 5, 10, 20, 50, and 100 subintervals, with
500, 5000, and 20000 samples.
(b) Convergence of S1 (dashed line) and S2
for 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, and 100 subintervals, optimised using differential evolution.
(c) Convergence of S1 (dashed line) and S2 for
