Fig. 3.6 Convergence comparison of the interval-based sensitivity index computed with
sampling, optimisation, and interval arithmetic.
3.4.2 Pinching
The objective of sensitivity analysis is not always to rank the input variables by their impact on
the output uncertainty. Sometimes the analyst is interested in investigating how much output
uncertainty can be removed after reducing the uncertainty of an input. This technique can
be particularly useful when the objective of the analysis is to inform research prioritisation,
for example, to find which input parameter is more efficient to invest resources to reduce
its uncertainty. In the imprecise probability community this has received the name of

62 Sensitivity Analysis
pinching [173, 74], since the technique involves the reduction of an interval to a precise point
value (hence the pinch). But note that this has been also investigated outside the realm of
imprecise probabilities, as in [167], where the output uncertainty reduction is an average
reduction of its variance when the variance of certain input is reduced.
The pinching method measures the reduction of uncertainty on the output if extra knowledge about the inputs is acquired. The initial step is to calculate the output uncertainty with
all the input parameters being uncertain. Then, an iterative process is carried out, consisting
in reducing the uncertainty of each input parameter to a point value (or tighter interval), and
measuring the output uncertainty after performing pinching for that parameter. The score for
each i
th input parameter is calculated as
Scorei = 1−
U(y)i
U(y)Total
,
where U(y)iis the measure of the output uncertainty when the uncertainty of input parameter
i has been reduced while keeping all the other input parameters uncertain, and U(y)Total
is the measure of the output uncertainty when all the input parameters are uncertain. The
greater the score is, the greater the output uncertainty reduction achieved.
A measure U(y) that is often employed in the framework of imprecise probabilities is
the Wasserstein distance, also called area metric [179]. When p-boxes are employed to
describe uncertainty,4the Wasserstein distance quantifies the area between the upper and
4A p-box is defined by three constraints: having two bounding cumulative distribution functions (CDFs),
interval bounds on the mean and variance, and a collection of distribution families.
Fig. 3.7 Comparison of f(x1, x2) = −4x1 +0x2 +ε with x1 ∈ [−5,5], x2 ∈ [−5,5] and ε ∼ N (0, x1),
computed with 20 subintervals using sampling, optimisation, and interval arithmetic.

3.4 Interval-based global sensitivity analysis 63
lower cumulative distribution functions (CDFs). Mathematically, it is defined as [180]
d(y) = Z ∞
−∞
|F(y)−F(y)|dy . (3.6)
When dealing with intervals, a specific instance of p-boxes, the Wasserstein distance
simplifies notably. For an interval y ∈ [y, y], the Wasserstein distance reduces to the width of
the interval. Hence, it is expressed as
d(y) = y−y . (3.7)
This simplification arises because, in the case of intervals, the difference between the upper
and lower CDFs becomes a constant value over the interval, equal to the interval’s width.
The methodology presented in [173] needs the specification of precise reductions for
implementing the pinching process. However, the justification for these point values can
sometimes be challenging, potentially introducing a degree of arbitrariness. Additionally,
there may be scenarios where pinching the same parameter at different points is desirable.
This process must be repeated individually for each combination of pinched parameter and
point value. In contrast, the interval-based approach discussed in this chapter eliminates the
need to select arbitrary values and does not require repetitive analysis for each parameter. The
same data used for uncertainty and sensitivity analysis can also provide pinching information.
This aspect is particularly beneficial in the context of computationally expensive models.
The pinching information is simply obtained calculating the width of the output for each
subinterval, as illustrated in Figure 3.8.
Fig. 3.8 Pinching of x1 and x2 in f(x1, x2) = −4x1 +0x2 +ε, computed with 20 subintervals using
sampling, optimisation, and interval arithmetic.

64 Sensitivity Analysis
3.4.3 Properties
This proposed method exhibits several essential properties, aligning closely with the ideal
characteristics of an effective sensitivity analysis. It satisfies Rényi’s Postulate 2, ensuring
symmetry. This method also adheres to Postulates 3, 4, and 5, being a measure that is
bounded within the [0,1] interval. A value of 0 indicates complete independence between
input xi and output y, and a value of 1 implies a strict deterministic relationship. This bounded
range provides analysts with clear, interpretable metrics. Moreover, the method is designed
with a global perspective rather than local, assessing sensitivity not just around nominal input
values but across the entire input space. This feature allows for a comprehensive evaluation
of potential input variations and their effects on outputs, making it particularly effective for
complex models (e.g. non-linear, non-monotonic, etc.).
The interval-based global sensitivity analysis is also integrated with uncertainty quantification. It leverages the same input samples used for uncertainty quantification, reducing
the need for additional simulations. This can be advantageous when dealing with computationally demanding models. The method is also tailored to handle epistemic uncertainty,
operating with minimal assumptions about the input’s uncertainty, which is appropriate in
situations where information is imprecise or not available. Another advantage is its capacity
of obtaining the results of pinching analysis without having to define specific pinching values
or to run additional simulations.
In conclusion, the interval-based sensitivity analysis is applicable to a wide range of
scenarios and models, easily interpretable, global, compatible with uncertainty quantification,
and capable of dealing with epistemic uncertainty.
3.5 Comparison with Sobol’ indices
To show the performance of the proposed interval-based global sensitivity analysis method,
its results in terms of parameter ranking are compared with the Sobol’ indices on the Ishigami
function, which is a common benchmark function in the uncertainty quantification and
sensitivity analysis community for its non-linearity, nonmonotonicity, and the interaction
effects between x1 and x3 [181]. The Ishigami function can be written as
f(x1, x2, x3) = sin(x1) +asin2(x2) +bsin(x1)x
4
3
, (3.8)
where the constants are set to a = 5 and b = 0.1 with x1, x2, x3 ∈ [−π,π].
Since the analytical description of the function is known, the interval-based sensitivity
analysis can be performed through interval arithmetic. Figure 3.9 shows the results of the

3.5 Comparison with Sobol’ indices 65
interval analysis of the Ishigami function with 100 subintervals for x1, x2, and x3, with their
corresponding sensitivity indices indicated in Table 3.1, calculated following the methodology
presented in Section 3.4. According to the interval-based method, the Ishigami function has
the highest dependence with x3, followed by x1.
Figure 3.10 shows the uncertainty on the Ishigami output when pinching x1, x2, and x3.
The maximum reduction on the Ishigami uncertainty is achieved when x1 is fixed to −π, 0,
or π. If pinching any of the three parameters to a point value were not possible, the second
best strategy to maximise the output uncertainty reduction would be to pinch the x3 interval
from [−π,π] to [−1,1], as Figure 3.10c suggests. Lastly, pinching x2 entails almost the same
uncertainty reduction, and the smallest of the three parameters, across its entire domain.
When the inputs of the Ishigami function are independent and uniformly distributed in
[−π,π], the analytical description of the variance terms used to calculate the Sobol’ indices
are known; therefore the exact first order and total Sobol’ indices can be calculated in this
case. Table 3.1 contains the analytical Sobol’ indices of the Ishigami function. Analytically,
the variance of x1 is the greatest contributor to the variance of the Ishigami function, and x3
has effect on the Ishigami only when interacting with x1.
A second case for the Ishigami function has been included where the input variables
follow a triangular distribution in [−π,π] with mode in 0. This example is used to support
the case when the distribution functions of the input variables are not totally known, showing
that the Sobol’ indices could be sensible to these assumptions. To calculate the Sobol’ indices
for the triangular setting, 2048 samples were generated using the Saltelli sampling method,
which is an extension of the Sobol’ sequence optimised for calculating the indices [182].
Table 3.1 shows the results of the analysis. The results suggest that, in this case, the variance
of x2 is the greatest contributor to the variance of the Ishigami. Note that the uncertainties on
the indices have been omitted since these were negligible, and had no impact on the final
parameter ranking.
