and unfeasible samples.
Results of the pinching analysis are summarised in Table 3.5 The parameters with the
most significant impact on reducing uncertainty in the major radius are plasma elongation,
the fraction of thermal 4He, and the H-factor, which decrease the uncertainty to 0.86, 1.138,
and 1.175 metres respectively, as illustrated in Figure 3.15. Although pinching the plasma
elongation within 1.799 and 1.809 yields the highest reduction in major radius uncertainty, the
major radius is expected to exceed 9.0 metres within this elongation interval. Consequently,
focusing on the thermal 4He fraction or the H-factor may be a more effective strategy. In
contrast, tungsten impurity fraction, core radius, and divertor operational limit contribute
minimally to the uncertainty reduction, indicating their lesser relevance in this context
(Figure 3.16).
3.7 Conclusion
This chapter introduces a novel method for performing global sensitivity analysis with
interval analysis, computed with sampling methods, optimisation, or interval arithmetic.

3.7 Conclusion 75
Table 3.5 Results of the pinching analysis.
