This method only requires expressing the input parameter uncertainty as intervals, being
particularly suited for scenarios under epistemic uncertainty. As a positive by-product, this
method also retrieves all the information of the pinching method, which measures the output
uncertainty reduction when input uncertainty is reduced to a point value or subinterval.
The method is applied on the Ishigami function, and its results were compared with
Sobol’ indices. When there is lack of knowledge about the probability distribution function
of the inputs, variance-based methods such as Sobol’ indices can return contradictory results.
This effect was illustrated using uniform and triangular distribution functions for the inputs
of the Ishigami. In this respect, when the inputs are not precisely known the interval-based
approach is more reliable since it returns a unique ranking for a given input domain.
An application to the European 2018 H-mode DEMO baseline is also shown. The
results suggest that, from the parameters considered, H-factor, thermal 4He fraction, and
plasma elongation are the most influential on the major radius. Also, results from the
pinching analysis indicate that the uncertainty of the major radius is minimised when reducing
uncertainty of the plasma elongation from [1.75, 1.90] to [1.799, 1.809]. Furthermore, plasma
elongation and thermal 4He fraction shown the highest impact on the feasibility of DEMO.
Initial analyses suggests that this is due to the influence of the plasma elongation on the
fusion power, and the thermal 4He fraction on the energy confinement time.

