Once the reaction type is determined, the products of the reaction are elucidated and the
neutron energy and direction are determined. Then, the process is repeated until the neutron
is absorbed or removed from the simulation. Finally, after a large number of neutron histories

80 Optimisation under Uncertainty
are tracked and the contribution of each neutron is recorded, the average behaviour of the
neutrons is inferred employing the central limit theorem, and physical quantities such as the
neutron flux, leakage, or energy deposition can be calculated.
Several codes exist to perform Monte Carlo transport calculations; some of them are
MCNP [196], OpenMC [197], Serpent [198], Geant4 [199], or Fluka [200].
4.1.1 Uncertainties in fusion neutronics
Neutronics simulations are subject to different sources of uncertainty related to modelling
assumptions, computation methods, and nuclear data [135].
Some of the assumptions required to solve the neutron transport problem with Monte
Carlo methods are the time-invariability and isotropy of materials. In a fusion reactor,
materials are subject to heat fluxes and radiation, which modify their structure and properties
over time [201]. Furthermore, material impurities are important for calculating their expected
activation after being exposed to radiation. It is known that the presence of silver, niobium,
and molybdenum impurities in steel can impede materials to comply with the low-activation
criterion for fusion [202].
Another source of uncertainty lies in the variability of the neutron source from the
deuterium-tritium plasma, which suffers from turbulence and other plasma physics effects.
Usually, a simplified description of the plasma is employed in neutronics analyses. In
these descriptions, thermal equilibrium of the plasma and a Maxwellian distribution around
14.1 MeV for the neutrons are assumed [203]. However, fusion reactors require external
plasma heating that affect the plasma temperature profile [204], and can potentially impact
the neutron emission profile, as well as the neutron energy spectra. In [205], a method was
developed to enable a realistic plasma source for fusion neutronics, which does take into
account the aforementioned effects.
There are other kinds of uncertainty that are inherent in neutronics, independent of the
modelling resources available. These are the statistical uncertainty of Monte Carlo methods
and nuclear data uncertainty. The statistical error of Monte Carlo is proportional to √
1
N
, thus
it can be reduced by increasing the number of simulated particles. Nonetheless, this solution
can be too costly when dealing with complex geometries and computationally demanding
simulations, so other approaches are necessary. An alternative is to employ variance reduction
techniques, such as MAGIC [206]. Note that crude Monte Carlo is rarely performed, and
variance reduction techniques are usually applied.
On the other hand, there is nuclear data uncertainty. Nuclear data is a fundamental input
to any nuclear calculation, since it defines all the complex particle-matter interaction physics.
Nuclear data can be collected in experiments where particles of different energies collide with

4.1 Neutronics 81
targets. The data has been systematically compiled since the discovery of the neutron, and
is available in databases such as the Experimental Nuclear Reaction Data (EXFOR) [207].
Then, the physical data is fitted (i.e. evaluated) using a combination of experimental data,
nuclear models, and human judgement. The evaluations are performed by different research
groups, and the results are published in libraries such as the Evaluated Nuclear Data File
(ENDF [208]), Japanese Evaluated Nuclear Library (JENDL [209]), Joint Evaluated Fission
and Fusion File (JEFF [210]), etc. For each nuclide-reaction pair, nuclear data libraries
usually provide at least three quantities:
• Interaction cross-sections: characterising probabilities of particular reactions,
• Exit angle/energy distributions: the output distribution of exit particles,
• Covariances: the variance, auto-correlation, and cross-section information between
data and quantities.
There are a few limitations that introduce epistemic uncertainty in the evaluation of
nuclear data. First is the lack of experimental data, specially for fusion applications. Figure 4.1 (left) shows all the EXFOR data available for the Pb(n,2n) reaction. This reaction
is particularly important for fusion applications since lead is considered in some tritium
breeding blanket designs for its neutron multiplication properties. In fact, it was found that
the uncertainty in tritium breeding with lithium lead blankets is due mainly to uncertainties
associated with multiplying reactions such as Pb(n,2n) and Pb(n,3n) [211]. However, only 21
datapoints from 6 experiments are available from the EXFOR database. As a comparison,
Figure 4.1 (right) shows the 235U fission cross-section data from 2 experiments stored in the
EXFOR database, one focusing on the resonance region, and another ranging from thermal
to 170 keV. These 2 experiments alone contain 8500 datapoints.
Fig. 4.1 EXFOR data for the Pb(n,2n) reaction (left) and 235U(n,f) reaction (right).

82 Optimisation under Uncertainty
Furthermore, there are different correlations that can be considered: between the different
energies in a single reaction, reactions on the same target, between various targets, etc.
Also, covariances on quantities such as energy and angular distribution are required for
the calculation of uncertainty in the cross-sections, but there is no standard of what is
necessary in all data libraries, and therefore it is complicated for this heterogeneous data to
be used consistently by application codes. As an example, for the Pb(n,2n) reaction only
the ENDF/B-V library provides correlation data that allows the calculation of uncertainty
in the cross-section, as shown in Figure 4.2. Ultimately, the evaluation of correlations and
uncertainties are subjective and will depend on the judgement and experience of the scientists
making the assessment. In the general case, as only the variances are provided in the nuclear
data, the uncertainty must be assumed to follow a multivariate distribution function (usually
a gaussian).
Fig. 4.2 Uncertainty in the cross-section data for the Pb(n,2n) reaction in the ENDF/B-V
library.
The lack of experimental data combined with the uncertainty in physics models and the
absence of a standard treatment of covariance in the data cause discrepancies between nuclear
data libraries. From Figure 4.3 it is possible to see that nuclear data libraries can be in better
agreement for well-studied fission-related isotopes than for fusion-relevant isotopes, as it is
the case for the 235U(n,f) and Pb(n,2n) reactions.
Recently, there has been a growing interest on the uncertainties in nuclear data, particularly in improving existing evaluations of covariances, or evaluating new covariances
which have been absent from past libraries. Figure 4.4 shows an increase in uncertainty data
over the past 30 years. More recently, Bayesian inference has been used with the nuclear
optical model, as is done in the TENDL data library [212], which does yield non-gaussian
evaluations. However, this library only provides raw sample data for a select few nuclides.
A more modern nuclear data format has been proposed (e.g. see [213]), which allows for

4.1 Neutronics 83
Fig. 4.3 Evaluated cross-section data for Pb(n,2n) reaction (left) and 235U(n,f) reaction
(right).
the probability distribution function to be specified, as well as covariances, which would
certainly improve the uncertainty quantification in nuclear applications.
Fig. 4.4 Amount of nuclear data classified by quantities since 1990 [214].
Nuclear data is essential for the calculation of relevant quantities in fusion reactor design,
such as tritium breeding, material damage, activation, or heat production. These quantities
will determine specific aspects of the reactor, and are crucial for the assessment of its safety,
economy, and maintainability. Therefore, the quality of nuclear data plays a critical role
in important engineering considerations. Traditionally, deficiencies in nuclear data have
been dealt with safety margins that make the reactors larger, more expensive, and less
efficient [215]. Being able to propagate nuclear data uncertainty in neutronics calculations,

84 Optimisation under Uncertainty
and being able to take it into account during the design optimisation process of fusion reactors
would result in safer and better-performing designs.
4.1.2 Nuclear data uncertainty propagation
One of the most popular methods for nuclear data uncertainty propagation combines adjoint
models and a local sensitivity analysis [216], sometimes called perturbation methods. Here,
the sensitivity matrix of the output response is found by computing their derivatives with
respect to the nuclear data inputs. The variances of the output response can then be found by
a simple matrix transformation of the input variances and the computed sensitivity matrix,
sometimes called the sandwich rule. Once the sensitivity matrix has been computed, any
nuclear data quantity can be propagated with this method, including data from different
libraries without re-computing the sensitivity matrix.
There are some assumptions that are required for perturbation methods, however. Firstly,
only the output mean and variance are computed, and not the entire distribution function.
Without additional distribution shape assumptions (e.g. normality), little can be reliably said
about the output uncertainty. Generally, the output of a non-linear function is non-gaussian
even if the inputs are. In [217], the authors argue that knowing the first two moments and
assuming normality can lead to overly confident answers. Additionally, in the absence of
the entire output distribution, a reliability analysis (i.e. a probability of failure calculation)
cannot be performed, and so may not be a suitable method for reliability-based design
optimisation. Secondly, the method is only accurate if the model is near-linear, or the inputs
uncertainty is small enough for the model to be treated as linear in the range of interest. This
may be appropriate for fission applications, whose relevant nuclides have been extensively
well studied, and have low uncertainties and accurate covariances. Nuclides that need to be
considered for fusion applications can be more diverse and often more exotic, with often
little experimental data, and large or unknown covariance evaluations [218].
As computational resources have become more available, Monte Carlo methods have
been become more accessible for nuclear data uncertainty quantification. In the absence of
raw random evaluations (as done in Total Monte Carlo [219]), random gaussian deviates can
be drawn from the nuclear data quantities, by first drawing correlated zero mean gaussian
samples from the covariance matrix (with Cholesky decomposition as usual choice) and
scaling it to the data’s mean function. The model is evaluated for each random nuclear
data sample, generating samples of the model’s output distribution. Figure 4.5 shows 500
samples randomly drawn from 208Pb’s elastic scattering cross-section, with the mean in blue
and the random samples in red. Monte Carlo does not suffer from the above mentioned

4.2 Optimisation under uncertainty 85
drawbacks, and can produce non-gaussian distributions for model responses, being suitable
for the evaluation of a design’s reliability.
Fig. 4.5 Left shows 500 random samples of 208Pb’s elastic scattering cross-section in red,
and the mean in blue. Right shows an expanded view of the resonance region.
4.2 Optimisation under uncertainty
Optimisation is an essential process in engineering design, as it deals with the problem of
finding the design that minimises certain figure of merit (e.g. cost) while satisfying some
constraints (e.g. safety margins). However, it is often the case that the system being optimised
is subject to different uncertainties, such as parameter uncertainty or model uncertainty.
Thus, to find safer and better-performing designs it would be desirable to account for those
uncertainties in the optimisation process.
Neutronics simulations are an essential part of fusion reactor design, as these are employed in the optimisation and performance assessments of some of its critical components,
such as the first wall and the breeding blanket [220, 221]. As mentioned before, neutronics
analyses are affected by statistical and nuclear data uncertainty. Thus, when dealing with
neutronics optimisation problems it seems reasonable to employ optimisation methods which
are capable of incorporating uncertainty.
4.2.1 Reliability analysis
Determining the probability of some nuclear components or systems failing to comply
with certain performance and safety requirements is of utmost importance for regulators,
stakeholders, and public. A design’s reliability can be assessed by computing the probability

86 Optimisation under Uncertainty
of the design not complying with its requirements given the uncertainties. For some failure
domain U , the region where the model does not meet its objectives, the model’s probability
of failure is
PX(U ) = Z
