That is, the model is randomly sampled, and the number of failed samples is counted.
Depending on the designer’s or regulator’s minimum risk level, the design may be accepted
as adequate, or must be further optimised to reduce the probability of failure, that is, to make
the design more robust against uncertainties.
Often, acceptable failure probabilities are very small (rare events). For example, in
aerospace engineering acceptable probabilities of failure are in the order of ∼ 10−6, and
potentially much lower in nuclear applications. With standard Monte Carlo, this would
require around a million simulations to (on average) get a sample in the failure domain, and so
rare-event simulations are often used, such as importance sampling or subset simulation [222].
In this work, however, basic Monte Carlo was employed to show that a deterministically
acceptable design can have a high probability of failure under nuclear data uncertainty. It
is worth mentioning that confidence intervals on the probability of failure PX can be found
from a single Monte Carlo simulation. For k failures out of n simulations, the two-sided
