fX(x)dx , (4.8)
that is, to find the design θ
∗
that reduces the amount of the output distribution inside the
failure domain. Clearly, wrapping a reliability calculation in an optimisation loop can be
computationally demanding, so an efficient optimisation method is needed for this procedure.
situations demanding high reliability, such as in certain areas of safety-critical engineering, erring on the side of
caution can be advantageous.

88 Optimisation under Uncertainty
4.2.3 Bayesian optimisation
Bayesian optimisation is a sequential model-based approach to solve optimisation problems,
which employs a probabilistic surrogate model of the objective function. This surrogate
model is a probabilistic approximation of the objective function, but considerably cheaper to
evaluate, and sequentially adjusted via Bayesian updating as new data are generated.
Along with the surrogate model, an acquisition function is employed to define the data
generation mechanism by leveraging the uncertainty in the surrogate model to guide the
exploration over the design space. The acquisition function measures the utility of candidate
designs for the next simulation of the objective function. The design with the highest utility is
simulated, and the surrogate model is updated with the new information. For a more detailed
explanation of the Bayesian optimisation framework see [224–226].
Bayesian optimisation is particularly appropriate to solve optimisation problems with
objective functions that are expensive to evaluate, behave as black-box, and return noisy
outputs. Neutronics simulations can be computationally demanding, especially when complex
geometries are analysed, so it is desirable to employ a methodology that requires the minimum
number of simulations to converge to a solution. Also, functions in neutronics simulations
generally do not have simple closed forms, and knowing their special structure (such as
concavity) or computing their derivative is not always possible, behaving as black-box
functions. Lastly, the outputs in neutronics simulations can carry statistical uncertainty
when Monte Carlo methods are employed, or when nuclear data uncertainties are considered.
For these reasons, the framework of Bayesian optimisation is well suited to neutronics
optimisation problems.
A popular approach for generating the surrogate model of the objective function employs
Gaussian process (GP) regression [227]. One of the advantages of GP surrogates is that they
provide the predicted value of unobserved data and an estimate of the prediction variance,
which gives information about the uncertainty in the surrogate model. The surrogate g(x) of
the objective function f(x) is constructed as
g(x) ∼ N(µ(x),K(x, x
′
)) , (4.9)
where µ is the mean vector of the observed data, and K is the covariance function, or kernel,
that models the uncertainty between observations in the GP surrogate.
Once the GP surrogate model is created, the acquisition function will determine the
mechanism for selection the designs that will be evaluated on the objective function. A
well-known acquisition function is the expected improvement (EI), which is defined as

4.3 Application 89
EI(x) = (f
∗ − µ(x))Φ(Z) +σ(x)φ(Z) , (4.10)
where f
∗
is the minimum observed value of f , µ(x) and σ(x) are the mean and standard
deviation of the GP surrogate at x, Φ and φ are the CDF and PDF of the standard normal distribution respectively, with Z = (f
∗−µ(x))/σ(x). The first and second term of Equation (4.10)
are often referred as exploitation and exploration respectively. EI balances between the
evaluation of solutions which are likely to have a low mean and the exploration of others
with high uncertainty. This method can provide a sufficient coverage of the design space,
while converging towards the region with high likelihood of being optimal. A pseudocode of
the algorithm for performing Bayesian optimisation is described in Algorithm 1.
Algorithm 1: Pseudocode for Bayesian optimisation
Define n number of initial samples and N number of EI samples
Generate x1, x2,..., xn points
Observe f(x1), f(x2),..., f(xn)
Train a GP surrogate model with [(x1, f1),(x2, f2),...,(xn, fn)]
while i ≤ N do
Find xi = argmax EI(x)
Observe f(xi)
Update the GP surrogate model with (xi, fi)
end
Return the solution observed with the lowest f(x)
4.3 Application
The main objective of the application is to investigate whether nuclear data uncertainty is
likely to have an impact on the reliability of fusion systems. To do so, a simplified fusion
reactor model will be designed to minimise its probability of not complying with some
requirements, with and without considering nuclear data uncertainty, and these designs will
be compared. The methodology employed combines OpenMC [197], nuclear data uncertainty
propagation methods [228], and Bayesian optimisation [224].
4.3.1 Problem setting
The optimisation problem is based on a three-layer spherical model as indicated in Figure 4.7.
At the core of the model there is a 2 cm diameter sphere acting as a deuterium-tritium neutron
source, followed by a first layer of tungsten with a thickness of (R1 − 1) cm. The next

90 Optimisation under Uncertainty
layer is a lithium-lead tritium breeding blanket parameterised with R2, and the last layer
is made of Eurofer97 acting as structural material. The lithium employed in the breeding
blanket is made of 50% 6Li and 50% 7Li, and Eurofer97 composition follows its target
values from [229]. The three-layer spherical model will serve as a toy problem, simple
enough to be evaluated with moderate computational power, but sufficiently sophisticated to
show the potential consequences of nuclear data uncertainty and the benefits of the proposed
methodology. The composition of the model has been defined to resemble a simplified fusion
reactor, with materials often envisaged for fusion power plants, such as tungsten for plasma
facing components, lithium-lead blanket for tritium breeding, and Eurofer97 as structural
material [100]. The Matérn kernel was employed to construct the Gaussian process surrogate
model.
Fig. 4.7 Diagram of the three-layered spherical model used for the design optimisation
problem. Variables R1 and R2 control the thicknesses of the layers.
The design must satisfy the following performance and safety requirements:
1. the tritium breeding ratio (TBR)2 must be at least 1.05,
2. the neutron leakage has to be lower than 0.25.
2
It is the ratio between tritium fuel produced in the blanket and tritium burned in the plasma. The TBR is a
fundamental figure of merit for the design and operation of fusion power plants due to their requirement of
tritium self-sufficiency.

4.3 Application 91
Furthermore, the solution must satisfy some geometric constraints. The layer of structural
material needs to have a minimum thickness fo 1 cm, the tungsten layer cannot be inside the
neutron source sphere, and R1 cannot be greater than R2.
The objective of the optimisation problem is to find the design R1,R2 ∈ [1,99] cm with
the minimum probability of failure, while satisfying the geometric constraints.
The simulations were carried out using the OpenMC Monte Carlo code [197], and
the optimisation routine used the scikit-optimize Python library [230]. To account for
nuclear data uncertainty, the cross-sections were sampled using SANDY [228] and processed
with NJOY2021 [231]. As many uncertain nuclides as possible were considered that had
covariances available. The following nuclides were randomly perturbed assuming stochastic
independence between them: 204Pb, 206Pb, 207Pb, 208Pb, 54Fe, 56Fe, 57Fe, 6Li, and 7Li were
sampled from ENDF/B-VII and 180W, 182W, 183W, 184W, and 186W from TENDL-2019 (as
their covariances were unavailable in ENDF/B-VII). ENDF/B-VII was employed as the
baseline library when the optimisation was performed without nuclear data uncertainty. The
OpenMC simulations were run in 10 batches of 1000 particles.
4.3.2 Results
Without nuclear data uncertainty
In this setting only the statistical uncertainty of the Monte Carlo simulation was taken into
account. Figure 4.8 shows the results of the optimisation process. The original design space
was initially explored, and a region of promising designs was found around R1 = 15cm
and R2 = 90cm (left of Figure 4.8). Then, a second optimisation was carred out focusing
on that region for a better inspection of the design space, setting it for R1 ∈ [1,20] cm and
R2 ∈ [40,99] cm (right of Figure 4.8). The optimum was found at R1 = 14.004cm and
R2 = 96.723cm with a failure probability of 0.0.
In regard to the Monte Carlo statistical uncertainty of the simulations, the standard
deviation fell in the intervals [3.4×10−3,8.7×10−3] and [1.6×10−3,4.4×10−3] for the
TBR and leakage respectively. As for the optimum fround, its TBR was 1.115 with a standard
deviation of 0.006, and a leakage of 0.214 with a 0.003 standard deviation. These results
suggest that 10 batches of 1000 particles were enough to achieve a reasonably small statistical
uncertainty.
Once the candidate was found, its probability of failure was calculated taking nuclear
data uncertainty into account, using 500 cross-section samples. The analysis returned a
probability of failure of 0.176 with the 95% confidence interval being [0.145,0.215]. These
results suggest that nuclear data uncertainty does indeed have an impact on the reliability

92 Optimisation under Uncertainty
Fig. 4.8 Gaussian process surrogate model of the failure probability without nuclear data
uncertainty over the original design space (left) and the promising region (right). Each dot
represents a simulated design. The red star is the observed optimum.
assessment of the model under consideration, since its probability of failure increases
significantly, which can have serious consequences in more detailed and complex designs.
With nuclear data uncertainty
After demonstrating that nuclear data uncertainty can have a significant impact on the
probability of failure, the optimisation algorithm has been adapted to account for it. For computational reasons, the number of cross-section samples were set to 500 in the optimisation
loop. A greater number of samples would return a more accurate assessment of the failure
probability.
Figure 4.9 shows the results of the optimisation process on the promising region identified
previously. It is possible to see how this region has expanded because of the nuclear data
uncertainty, smoothing the failure probability. Now the designs are more unlikely to return a
failure probability of 0 or 1. The reason is that the impact of nuclear data uncertainty on the
TBR and leakage uncertainty is greater than the Monte Carlo statistical uncertainty which
was present in the section before.
The optimum design found with nuclear data uncertainty was R1 = 11.496cm and
R2 = 93.311cm with a failure probability of 0.002 with the 95% confidence interval being
[4.84×10−4,1.44×10−2], which is a significantly more reliable design than the previously
found without nuclear data uncertainty.
Figure 4.10 shows the different output distribution for the optimised design with and
without considering nuclear data uncertainty. Clearly, when it was incorporated in the design

4.4 Conclusion 93
Fig. 4.9 Gaussian process surrogate model of the failure probability with nuclear data
uncertainty over the promising region.
optimisation process, the predicted TBR and leakage were less likely to fall in the failure
domain, giving a more reliable candidate design.
4.4 Conclusion
Neutron transport can be calculated employing Monte Carlo methods and interaction crosssections. These calculations are affected by the inherent statistical uncertainty of Monte
Carlo methods, but also by the uncertainty present in the nuclear data. Reliability-based
design optimisation can incorporate these uncertainties in the design optimisation process of
fusion reactors, resulting into safer and better-performing designs.
Reliability-based design optimisation problems are often expensive, and a computationally efficient strategy is required for solving them. Bayesian optimisation can help to reduce
the computational burden of these design optimisation problems, employing a surrogate
model that is fast to evaluate in combination with an acquisition function that allows the
exploration of the design space in an efficient manner.
In this chapter, a methodology to perform reliability-based design optimisation in neutronics problems was presented. This methodology is capable of incorporating nuclear data
uncertainty in the neutroncis analyses and finding optimal solutions in a computationally
efficient way through Bayesian optimisation. The results of this chapter indicate that not
considering nuclear data uncertainty in the design activities of fusion power plants could
have important consequences when assessing their probability of failure. When nuclear
data uncertainty was ignored during the design optimisation, but taken into account when
analysing the design’s reliability, the probability of not complying with the requirements

94 Optimisation under Uncertainty
Without nuclear data uncertainty
With nuclear data uncertainty
Fig. 4.10 Scatterplots of the optimal designs found with and without nuclear data uncertainty.
The design with nuclear data uncertainty has less samples inside the failure domain, hence a
lower probability of failure.

4.4 Conclusion 95
was [0.145,0.215]. However, when nuclear data uncertainty was explicitly considered in the
design optimisation, the probability of failure dropped to [4.84×10−4,1.44×10−2].
It is worth mentioning that the Monte Carlo statistical uncertainty and the nuclear data
uncertainty were not considered together in the optimisation loop. The results suggest that
when sufficient particles are simulated, the statistical uncertainty has a negligible impact on
the neutronics uncertainty compared to the nuclear data uncertainty.



Chapter 5
Risk Analysis
Preface The work presented in this chapter was introduced at the 19th International Conference on Information Processing and Management of Uncertainty in Knowledge-Based
Systems and published in its corresponding proceedings [232].
This chapter presents an AND gate which may be used to propagate uncertainty and dependence through Boolean functions, since any Boolean function may be expressed as a
combination of AND and NOT operations. It is argued that the proposed AND gate is a
bivariate copula family [233], which has the interpretation of constructing bivariate Bernoulli
random variables following a given Pearson correlation coefficient and marginal probabilities.
This copula family can be employed to propagate uncertainty in the form of probabilities
of events, probability intervals, and probability boxes, with only partial and no knowledge
of the dependency between events, expressed as an interval for the correlation coefficient.
These results generalise previous results by Fréchet on the conjunction of two events with
unknown dependencies. An application is shown propagating uncertainty through a fault tree
for a pressure tank. The developed methodology and its associated computational tools have
been released as an open-source Julia library, accessible for public use and collaboration.
5.1 Introduction
The logical conjunction (∧) is a function f : {0,1}
2 → {0,1} that returns a value of 1 if
and only if both of the inputs are 1. The logical values assigned to the inputs represent the
truth value of certain propositions or events, and Boolean algebra requires absolute certainty
about these truth values (i.e. the are either true (1) or false (0)). That is, variables take the
form p ∈ {0,1}. However, this requirement is often too restrictive to be used in practical

98 Risk Analysis
applications where truth values have some degree of uncertainty, and therefore an extension
of classical Booleans to uncertain Booleans would be desirable. Uncertain Booleans can be
defined as probabilities p ∈ [0,1] defining a precise Bernoulli distribution (an event), interval
probabilities p ⊆ [0,1] defining a set of Bernoulli distributions (a credal set), and probability
boxes with range(p) ⊆ [0,1]. This chapter presents a method to perform Boolean operations
with these structures.
Note that it is possible to build any binary Boolean operation from two primitive operations, for example from AND and NOT. Table 5.1 shows the 16 (2
4
) possible binary and
unary Boolean operations, written in terms of two primitives operations ∧ and ¬. Note that
some of these operations (e.g. Identity and Zero) are trivial, and with the other operations
being written in terms of previously derived operations for brevity. Since all of these operations may be written in terms of an ∧ and a ¬ operation, and since the NOT operation is
not(A) = ¬A = 1−A, it follows that only the description of a correlated AND operator is
needed, and all other binary operators, and further more complicated Boolean functions, may
be calculated in terms of these two operators.
