5.6 Conclusion
In this chapter, classical Boolean functions have been generalised to operate with precise probabilities, intervals, p-boxes, and with any input correlation. An application is shown for the
calculation of the probability of failure of a pressure tank system. In this example, the uncertainties are propagated through a fault tree with different combinations of probabilities of the
events, intervals, and p-boxes, under different dependence assumptions. The results suggest
that assumptions on the probability or dependence of the events can have a strong impact on
the outcome of the risk analysis, and these should be carefully addressed in any serious assessment. Furthermore, the issue of the two levels of dependence has been introduced, and shown
that it can have a dramatic effect on the results of a probabilistic risk assessment. The computational resources to perform correlated Boolean operations are available in the following opensource Julia package: https://github.com/Institute-for-Risk-and-Uncertainty/UncLogic.jl.

Chapter 6
Conclusions
6.1 Overview
The objectives of this thesis were to develop methodologies for characterising and quantifying
uncertainty in fusion power plant design activities. Traditional methodologies predominantly
rely on probability theory, which successfully addresses aleatory uncertainty but falls short in
capturing epistemic uncertainty due to its rigid assumptions. Designing and building a fusion
power plant is a completely new endeavour, and there are many epistemic uncertainties
regarding its design variables, the design itself, and the consequences of failure. Thus, it
became imperative to devise new methods capable of dealing with epistemic uncertainty.
Engineering design processes entail a series of activities, including uncertainty quantification,
sensitivity analysis, optimisation, and risk analysis. In this thesis, a methodology for each of
these activities was presented.
Chapter 3 focused on uncertainty quantification and sensitivity analysis. These activities
serve different purposes, including model verification, validation, calibration, reduction,
and helping decision-making processes. Existing methodologies proved inadequate for the
requirements of fusion power plant design, primarily due to their reliance on unreasonable
assumptions and their propensity to return contradictory outputs when addressing epistemic
uncertainty. Therefore, the development of a novel approach leveraging imprecise probabilities was required. An interval-based global sensitivity analysis method was derived, requiring
no assumptions about the input parameters other than their specified ranges.
Chapter 4 addressed the challenges of design optimisation of tritium breeding blankets
through reliability-based design optimisation. The design and analysis of breeding blankets
involve neutronics simulations, which are subject to different sources of uncertainty. Among
these, nuclear data uncertainty stands out in fusion applications due to experimental imprecision and data scarcity. Although the impact of nuclear data uncertainty in fusion systems has

112 Conclusions
been addressed previously (e.g. see [135] and [245]), a systematic optimisation framework
that incorporates these uncertainties into design optimisation activities was missing. The
methodology developed in Chapter 4 fills this gap by integrating nuclear data uncertainty
into the optimisation processes for breeding blankets, capable of finding more robust designs
when nuclear data uncertainty is propagated through neutronics models.
Chapter 5 explored the influence of epistemic uncertainty on probabilistic risk assessments, with a particular focus on fault-tree analysis. Fault trees employ Boolean gates to
propagate failure rates and calculate the probability of the top event. Conventionally, this
approach relies on precise probabilities for failure rates, which are based on available operational data and expert opinion. However, this reliance on data becomes a significant limitation
in the context of fusion power plants, where data scarcity leads to imprecise failure rate estimations. To address this challenge, Chapter 5 developed an extension of Boolean functions
to incorporate epistemic uncertainty, enhancing the applicability of fault-tree analysis in
scenarios characterised by incomplete data.
In summary, the main contributions of this thesis are:
1. A global sensitivity analysis method based on interval analysis that only requires
expressing the input parameter uncertainty as intervals.
2. An optimisation framework for fusion neutronics that combines nuclear data uncertainty propagation methods, reliability-based design optimisation, and Bayesian
optimisation.
3. An extension of logic gates that can operate with precise probabilities, intervals, and
p-boxes, with any input correlation.
Incorporating epistemic uncertainty in engineering design activities is critical for advancing technology safely and efficiently. In fusion power plant design, where the technology is
complex and the stakes are high, neglecting epistemic uncertainty can lead to designs that fail
to comply with their requirements or, at worst, pose serious risks. These contributions overcome limitations of existing methods, offering an improvement for making better-informed
decisions and an advance towards transparency and honesty.
6.2 Directions for future research
The sensitivity analysis method presented in Chapter 3 encounters different challenges that
would benefit from further exploration. For example, it is unclear how the number of subintervals used to calculate the sensitivity index influences its accuracy. Empirical observations

6.2 Directions for future research 113
suggest that the index converges increasing this number, but the precise mathematics of the
relationship is not known. It is suspected, though, that the model also has an impact on
the convergence of the index, and a general relationship might not exist. This would be a
useful contribution to better understand how the index behaves with functions of different
kinds that are monotonic, non-monotonic, discontinuous, etc., and to find the appropriate
number of subintervals for a desired accuracy. A starting point could be testing the index
with the set of benchmark functions from [246]. Note that the index is currently calculated
through an outer approximation, but it could be technically possible to compute an inner
approximation too [247]; this would yield two bounds on the index which converge to the
exact index in both directions as more computation is performed. This would give a rigorous
calculation of the interval-based sensitivity index. Furthermore, this method requires interval
analysis for uncertainty propagation, performed with sampling, optimisation, or interval arithmetic. However, there are other methods for performing interval analysis, such as Cauchy
deviates [248], which have not been explored. It would also be interesting to investigate
how to calculate the index coupling interval refinement methods, such as the centered form
or mean value form [162], with interval arithmetic. These have the potential to compute
sharp bounds on the output more efficiently than the current subintervalisation method. In a
similar fashion, the SIVIA algorithm could be employed to get a more accurate measurement
of the index, specially on nonlinear functions [249]. Another direction for development
may involve adaptive subintervalisation, an approach similar to adaptive mesh refinement
found in finite element methods [250]. The current method divides the input intervals into
N subintervals of equal width, which may capture regions characterised by nonlinearities
inaccurately; an adaptive scheme that modifies subinterval lengths could produce, ceteris
paribus, more accurate sensitivity indices. Lastly, when sampling is employed to perform
interval analysis, using a quadtree [251] to measure the area of the scatterplot could be more
efficient than the current uniform subintervalisation.
As for the analysis of DEMO carried out in Chapter 3, a clear continuation of the work
would involve increasing the number of uncertain parameters or including engineering
parameters in the analysis as in [111]. Another interesting extension could be analysing the
impact of different energy confinement time scaling laws on the size and performance of
DEMO. The application included in Chapter 3 was performed with the so-called IPB98(y,2)
scaling law [33], but PROCESS can run simulations with other scaling laws from the
literature, such as the non-power law [252], ITPA20 [108], or [253], among others. A similar
argument can be made about the uncertainties of other DEMO design drivers, such as the
temperature margin for high-temperature superconducting magnets, or the heat protection
and erosion limits for divertor plates [254].

114 Conclusions
In Chapter 4, a simplified fusion reactor model was employed, which could raise concerns about the validity of the findings under realistic reactor designs, which feature more
complex geometries and material compositions. The extent to which nuclear data uncertainty influences the performance of breeding blankets in more realistic models is not totally
understood [255]. Greater complexity in geometry and materials may either mitigate or
amplify the impact of nuclear data uncertainty. This remains an open question and exploring
this issue through simulations involving more detailed reactor models would constitute
a valuable direction for future research. Another possible extension of this work would
involve inventory calculations. The objective of inventory calculations is to analyse the timedependent isotope densities of materials after being subject to neutron-induced activation and
transmutation [256]. This allows the calculation of decay heat, particle spectrum, shutdown
dose rates, etc., that need to be managed during reactor operation and decommissioning.
Integrating inventory calculations in the optimisation framework would allow finding designs that comply with activation and radioactive waste requirements. Furthermore, in the
context of computational efficiency, the method presented in Chapter 4 can be costly due
to its reliance on douple-loop Monte Carlo simulations. Its computational burden could
be alleviated by employing an acquisition function that balances not only the exploration
and exploitation of the design space but also the computational cost of each simulation,
as in cost-aware Bayesian optimisation [257]. Note that a cost-aware acquisition function
can also be applied to design experiments within a fixed or limited budget, which could
be useful for an experimental programme with an available cost model. In this regard, it
would be interesting to see an application of Bayesian optimisation and machine learning
for guiding tritium breeding blanket design activities, in a similar way that these are applied
for optimising target designs in inertial confinement fusion experiments [258–260] or for
designing experiments to improve nuclear data [261, 262].
With respect to the Boolean operators introduced in Chapter 5, these were limited to two
inputs, a constraint that can be too restrictive when dealing with real applications, which often
require dealing with gates of multiple inputs. While it is possible to decompose multiple-input
gates into two-input gates, the implications of such decomposition on the correlated Boolean
operators are still unclear. To begin with, it is unknown how the correlation would have to be
decomposed with the inputs. Additionally, the decomposition would cause repeated variables,
which aggravate the dependency problem associated with interval arithmetic, potentially
leading to an overestimation of the output interval. Furthermore, Chapter 5 showed that the
correlated Boolean operators are capable of dealing with two levels of dependence. The
lower-level dependence can be captured by correlation coefficients, which can be estimated
from data. On the other hand, the upper-level dependence is, in general, modelled using

6.2 Directions for future research 115
copulas and it is unclear how to estimate these copulas. It was shown that the upper-level
dependence can have a dramatic impact on the results, thus warranting further investigation
on how to better capture it. With respect to the application, this chapter would have benefited
from using a fault tree and component operational data from fusion facilities, such as that
contained in the Fusion Component Failure Rate Database (FCFR-DB) [143].
As a final remark for fusion power plant design, a straightforward direction towards
future research is the eventual construction of a fusion power plant. However, there is a
significant gap between the current understanding of nuclear fusion as an energy source and
the required knowledge to build and operate a successful fusion power plant. Numerous
sub-systems still require further validation before they can be integrated into a fusion power
plant. To bridge this gap, intermediate facilities between current experimental machines and
DEMO are required. This thesis introduced examples of such facilities, including ITER and
IFMIF-DONES. Although the models of sub-systems involving these intermediate facilities
can be validated individually [27](e.g. see [263–265]), their integration into a comprehensive
fusion power plant model remains unvalidated, as no operational fusion power plant currently
exists. It is important to recall, however, that the integration of such sub-systems into fusion
systems codes can still provide useful insights by adding numerical values to scenarios,
provided that the assumptions for the extrapolation are properly disclosed and understood.
The question of what intermediate machine should be built next is critical, and the answer
may vary depending on the expertise and budget of the organisation consulted. Since the
discovery of the nuclear fusion reaction several reactors of a different kind have been built:
magnetic mirrors, pinch devices, stellarators, and tokamaks [266]. Additionally, different
plasma control methods [267], plasma shapes [268], and plasma regimes [269] were devised.
Advanced plasma start-up techniques [270, 271] and disruption mitigation methods [272]
have also been explored. Improvements in magnet technology [273] and remote maintenance
strategies [274] were made. Alternative divertor configurations [88, 275], along with various
in-vessel components and structural materials [276], have been investigated too. Yet, there
is an essential sub-system that has never been tested: the tritium breeding blanket. A
programme to “test and qualify tritium breeding blanket technologies and design concepts
in a dedicated nuclear plasma device that serves as a 14 MeV volumetric neutron source”,
“was originally proposed more than 30 years ago but not implemented” [133]. A programme
of this scale would require a burning plasma to produce fusion-born neutrons, as well as
the evaluation and control of fuel cycles, tritium inventory management, and compliance
with tritium safety regulations. It would also involve fuel clean-up processes, including
fuel and isotope separation, exhaust detritiation, water detritiation, and coolant purification
systems [277]. Beyond that, the design and optimisation of the blanket would rely heavily

116 Conclusions
on simulation methods. In conclusion, a tritium breeding blanket programme requires
uncertainty quantification, sensitivity analysis, design optimisation under uncertainty, and
risk analysis. Consequently, the methodologies developed in this thesis would be beneficial
for such a programme and for the advancement of nuclear fusion as an energy source.

